INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation', '231d8cc57-stem1', '0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation/10', 10, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'review', ARRAY['error-handling-review', 'error-envelope', 'hide-by-default']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript/NestJS. Nhưng đọc code thì thấy mỗi handler trong controller tự bọc try/catch riêng của mình, mỗi catch tự trả về một JSON ad-hoc khác nhau. }. Không có gì được rethrow, cũng không có gì được log. Hãy review PR này ở tầm kiến trúc: điều gì sẽ vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần, rủi ro rò rỉ thông tin nằm ở đâu, và bạn đề xuất sửa thế nào?","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Controller('orders')\nexport class OrdersController {\n  constructor(private readonly orders: OrdersService) {}\n\n  @Get(':id')\n  async getOrder(@Param('id') id: string, @Res() res: Response) {\n    try {\n      const order = await this.orders.findById(id);\n      return res.status(200).json(order);\n    } catch (err) {\n      // shape #1 — trả thẳng message gốc, không log gì cả\n      return res.status(500).json({ message: (err as Error).message });\n    }\n  }\n\n  @Post()\n  async createOrder(@Body() dto: CreateOrderDto, @Res() res: Response) {\n    try {\n      const order = await this.orders.create(dto);\n      return res.status(201).json(order);\n    } catch (err) {\n      // shape #2 — envelope khác hẳn, vẫn không rethrow, không log\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: true, detail: (err as Error).message });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated with TypeScript/NestJS. But reading the code, every handler in the controller wraps its own try/catch, and each catch returns its own ad-hoc JSON. }. Nothing is rethrown, nothing is logged. Review this PR at the architecture level: what breaks as the system grows, where's the leak risk, and what do you propose?","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Controller('orders')\nexport class OrdersController {\n  constructor(private readonly orders: OrdersService) {}\n\n  @Get(':id')\n  async getOrder(@Param('id') id: string, @Res() res: Response) {\n    try {\n      const order = await this.orders.findById(id);\n      return res.status(200).json(order);\n    } catch (err) {\n      // shape #1 — returns the raw message as-is, nothing logged\n      return res.status(500).json({ message: (err as Error).message });\n    }\n  }\n\n  @Post()\n  async createOrder(@Body() dto: CreateOrderDto, @Res() res: Response) {\n    try {\n      const order = await this.orders.create(dto);\n      return res.status(201).json(order);\n    } catch (err) {\n      // shape #2 — a totally different envelope, still no rethrow, no log\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: true, detail: (err as Error).message });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"Bạn đang review một PR đã chạy và pass hết test. Nhưng đọc code thì thấy mỗi handler trong controller tự bọc try/catch riêng của mình, mỗi catch tự trả về một response body ad-hoc khác nhau. Không có gì được rethrow, cũng không có gì được log. Hãy review PR này ở tầm kiến trúc: điều gì sẽ vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần, rủi ro rò rỉ thông tin nằm ở đâu, và bạn đề xuất sửa thế nào?","givenCode":"```java\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/orders\")\npublic class OrdersController {\n\n  private final OrdersService orders;\n\n  public OrdersController(OrdersService orders) {\n    this.orders = orders;\n  }\n\n  @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n  public ResponseEntity<?> getOrder(@PathVariable String id) {\n    try {\n      Order order = orders.findById(id);\n      return ResponseEntity.ok(order);\n    } catch (Exception ex) {\n      // shape #1 — trả thẳng message gốc, không log gì cả\n      Map<String, String> body = new HashMap<>();\n      body.put(\"message\", ex.getMessage());\n      return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body(body);\n    }\n  }\n\n  @PostMapping\n  public ResponseEntity<?> createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderDto dto) {\n    try {\n      Order order = orders.create(dto);\n      return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(order);\n    } catch (Exception ex) {\n      // shape #2 — envelope khác hẳn, vẫn không rethrow, không log\n      Map<String, Object> body = new HashMap<>();\n      body.put(\"error\", true);\n      body.put(\"detail\", ex.getMessage());\n      return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(body);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. But reading the code, every handler in the controller wraps its own try/catch, and each catch returns its own ad-hoc response body. Nothing is rethrown, nothing is logged. Review this PR at the architecture level: what breaks as the system grows, where's the leak risk, and what do you propose?","givenCode":"```java\n@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/orders\")\npublic class OrdersController {\n\n  private final OrdersService orders;\n\n  public OrdersController(OrdersService orders) {\n    this.orders = orders;\n  }\n\n  @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n  public ResponseEntity<?> getOrder(@PathVariable String id) {\n    try {\n      Order order = orders.findById(id);\n      return ResponseEntity.ok(order);\n    } catch (Exception ex) {\n      // shape #1 — returns the raw message as-is, nothing logged\n      Map<String, String> body = new HashMap<>();\n      body.put(\"message\", ex.getMessage());\n      return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body(body);\n    }\n  }\n\n  @PostMapping\n  public ResponseEntity<?> createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderDto dto) {\n    try {\n      Order order = orders.create(dto);\n      return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(order);\n    } catch (Exception ex) {\n      // shape #2 — a totally different envelope, still no rethrow, no log\n      Map<String, Object> body = new HashMap<>();\n      body.put(\"error\", true);\n      body.put(\"detail\", ex.getMessage());\n      return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(body);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"Bạn đang review một PR đã chạy và pass hết test. Nhưng đọc code thì thấy mỗi endpoint tự bọc try/catch riêng của mình, mỗi catch tự trả về một object JSON ad-hoc khác nhau. Không có gì được rethrow, cũng không có gì được log. Hãy review PR này ở tầm kiến trúc: điều gì sẽ vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần, rủi ro rò rỉ thông tin nằm ở đâu, và bạn đề xuất sửa thế nào?","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic static class OrdersEndpoints\n{\n    public static void MapOrdersEndpoints(this WebApplication app)\n    {\n        app.MapGet(\"/orders/{id}\", async (string id, IOrdersService orders) =>\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                var order = await orders.FindByIdAsync(id);\n                return Results.Ok(order);\n            }\n            catch (Exception ex)\n            {\n                // shape #1 — trả thẳng message gốc, không log gì cả\n                return Results.Json(new { message = ex.Message }, statusCode: 500);\n            }\n        });\n\n        app.MapPost(\"/orders\", async (CreateOrderDto dto, IOrdersService orders) =>\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                var order = await orders.CreateAsync(dto);\n                return Results.Json(order, statusCode: 201);\n            }\n            catch (Exception ex)\n            {\n                // shape #2 — envelope khác hẳn, vẫn không rethrow, không log\n                return Results.Json(new { error = true, detail = ex.Message }, statusCode: 400);\n            }\n        });\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"You're reviewing a PR that runs and passes every test. But reading the code, every endpoint wraps its own try/catch, and each catch returns its own ad-hoc JSON object. Nothing is rethrown, nothing is logged. Review this PR at the architecture level: what breaks as the system grows, where's the leak risk, and what do you propose?","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic static class OrdersEndpoints\n{\n    public static void MapOrdersEndpoints(this WebApplication app)\n    {\n        app.MapGet(\"/orders/{id}\", async (string id, IOrdersService orders) =>\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                var order = await orders.FindByIdAsync(id);\n                return Results.Ok(order);\n            }\n            catch (Exception ex)\n            {\n                // shape #1 — returns the raw message as-is, nothing logged\n                return Results.Json(new { message = ex.Message }, statusCode: 500);\n            }\n        });\n\n        app.MapPost(\"/orders\", async (CreateOrderDto dto, IOrdersService orders) =>\n        {\n            try\n            {\n                var order = await orders.CreateAsync(dto);\n                return Results.Json(order, statusCode: 201);\n            }\n            catch (Exception ex)\n            {\n                // shape #2 — a totally different envelope, still no rethrow, no log\n                return Results.Json(new { error = true, detail = ex.Message }, statusCode: 400);\n            }\n        });\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"Bạn đang review một PR đã chạy và pass hết test. Nhưng đọc code thì thấy mỗi handler tự viết code xử lý lỗi riêng ngay tại chỗ err!= nil. Không handler nào gọi c.Error(err) để lỗi trôi tới middleware ErrorHandler tập trung, cũng không có gì được log. Hãy review PR này ở tầm kiến trúc: điều gì sẽ vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần, rủi ro rò rỉ thông tin nằm ở đâu, và bạn đề xuất sửa thế nào?","givenCode":"```go\npackage orders\n\nimport (\n\t\"net/http\"\n\n\t\"github.com/gin-gonic/gin\"\n)\n\nfunc GetOrder(c *gin.Context) {\n\tid := c.Param(\"id\")\n\torder, err := ordersService.FindByID(id)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// shape #1 — trả thẳng message gốc, không log gì cả\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusInternalServerError, gin.H{\"message\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tc.JSON(http.StatusOK, order)\n}\n\nfunc CreateOrder(c *gin.Context) {\n\tvar dto CreateOrderDto\n\tif err := c.ShouldBindJSON(&dto); err != nil {\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusBadRequest, gin.H{\"message\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\n\torder, err := ordersService.Create(dto)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// shape #2 — envelope khác hẳn, vẫn không c.Error(), không log\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusBadRequest, gin.H{\"error\": true, \"detail\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tc.JSON(http.StatusCreated, order)\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"You're reviewing a PR that runs and passes every test. But reading the code, every handler writes its own ad-hoc error-handling right at the err!= nil check. No handler calls c.Error(err) to let the error flow to a central ErrorHandler middleware, and nothing is logged. Review this PR at the architecture level: what breaks as the system grows, where's the leak risk, and what do you propose?","givenCode":"```go\npackage orders\n\nimport (\n\t\"net/http\"\n\n\t\"github.com/gin-gonic/gin\"\n)\n\nfunc GetOrder(c *gin.Context) {\n\tid := c.Param(\"id\")\n\torder, err := ordersService.FindByID(id)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// shape #1 — returns the raw message as-is, nothing logged\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusInternalServerError, gin.H{\"message\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tc.JSON(http.StatusOK, order)\n}\n\nfunc CreateOrder(c *gin.Context) {\n\tvar dto CreateOrderDto\n\tif err := c.ShouldBindJSON(&dto); err != nil {\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusBadRequest, gin.H{\"message\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\n\torder, err := ordersService.Create(dto)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// shape #2 — a totally different envelope, still no c.Error(), no log\n\t\tc.JSON(http.StatusBadRequest, gin.H{\"error\": true, \"detail\": err.Error()})\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tc.JSON(http.StatusCreated, order)\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu lỗi được ném ra hoàn toàn ngoài context của một request HTTP — ví dụ bên trong một background job — bạn giữ envelope nhất quán ở đó bằng cách nào?","Bạn đặt rule gì cho team: message text nào được coi là an toàn để đưa vào một lỗi hướng ra client, và message nào tuyệt đối không được rời khỏi server?"],"hints":["Tự hỏi: nếu hai handler tự bắt lỗi độc lập với nhau, ai thực sự là chủ sở hữu của \"shape\" một response lỗi — bản thân handler, hay một tầng nào đó ở trên?","Nhìn kỹ một handler tự bắt lỗi domain \"không tìm thấy\" cục bộ — nó luôn báo thành lỗi chung chung thay vì phản ánh đúng bản chất \"not found\". Thông tin gì đã bị vứt bỏ ngay khoảnh khắc `catch` chặn lỗi lại?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If the error is thrown entirely outside an HTTP request's context — say, inside a background job — how do you keep the response envelope consistent there too?","What rule do you actually set for the team: which message text is safe to put in a client-facing error, and which must never leave the server?"],"hints":["Ask yourself: if two handlers each catch their own errors independently, who actually owns the shape of an error response — the handler itself, or something above it?","Look closely at a handler that catches a domain \"not found\" error locally — it always reports back as a generic error instead of reflecting the real \"not found\" nature. What information got thrown away the instant `catch` intercepted it?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[technical] Chỉ đúng thứ bị bỏ qua: một `try/catch` riêng ở từng handler né hẳn tầng xử lý lỗi tập trung của framework (exception filter ở Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, hoặc middleware + `recover()` tập trung ở Go) — kết quả là mỗi handler tự quyết định shape lỗi của mình, trong khi lý do tồn tại của tầng tập trung chính là định nghĩa MỘT envelope duy nhất cho cả hệ thống.","[technical] Chỉ ra việc trả thẳng message gốc của exception ra client vi phạm hide-by-default: message gốc của một exception bất kỳ (driver DB, ORM...) có thể chứa tên bảng, tên cột, chi tiết câu query — những thứ tuyệt đối không nên lộ ra client.","[problemSolving] Nhận ra chỗ conflate expected vs unexpected: một lỗi domain \"not found\" đoán-trước-được lại bị báo thành lỗi 500 chung chung thay vì một status/shape riêng đúng nghĩa \"not found\"; và vì không log gì cả nên khi một lỗi thật sự bất ngờ xảy ra trên production, không còn dấu vết nào để truy — quan sát được (observability) của hệ thống coi như mất trắng.","[problemSolving] Đề xuất đúng hướng sửa: bỏ hẳn `try/catch` cục bộ ở handler, ném một lỗi có kiểu (typed) cho các case đoán-trước-được — một exception class có kiểu trên Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, hoặc một giá trị lỗi có kiểu (sentinel/typed error) trên Go — còn lại để rơi tự nhiên xuống MỘT điểm xử lý lỗi tập trung duy nhất đã tồn tại sẵn đúng cho việc này.","[communication] Review mang tính xây dựng: tách rõ cái BẮT BUỘC sửa (leak thông tin, mất observability) khỏi cái chỉ là style; giải thích được VÌ SAO — envelope là một contract nhất quán cho mọi client gọi API, không phải chuyện gu cá nhân của từng người viết handler.","[testing] Chứng minh bằng test cụ thể: ném một lỗi domain trong service, assert response trả về đúng shape envelope chuẩn của điểm xử lý lỗi tập trung, và assert body KHÔNG chứa bất kỳ đoạn text nào của message nội bộ gốc (vd message driver DB)."],"checklist":[{"text":"A per-handler try/catch that builds its own JSON error response duplicates work already owned by the framework's centralized error-handling layer (an exception filter on Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, or a central middleware plus recover() on Go) — the entire reason that layer exists is to define ONE response envelope for the whole system.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"When every handler catches its own errors, the response shape drifts across endpoints — one returns {message}, another returns {error, detail} — forcing clients to guess the format per endpoint instead of parsing a single contract.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Returning an exception's raw message straight to the client violates hide-by-default: that message belongs to whatever exception fired underneath — a DB driver, an ORM — and can carry table names, column names, or query fragments that must never reach outside the server.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Catching an expected domain error such as \"not found\" inside a local try/catch collapses it into the same generic response as any other failure, throwing away the information that would let it surface as a real 404 instead of a 500.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Swallowing an error without logging it removes any trace of the failure; when a genuinely unexpected error hits production, there is nothing left to grep for, so observability is effectively gone.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The correct fix is to strip the local try/catch out of the handlers entirely: throw a typed exception (NotFoundException, BadRequestException, or an equivalent typed error/sentinel per stack) for expected cases with a message the domain deliberately chose to expose, and let everything else fall through to the one centralized handler that already classifies, logs, and formats it.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Separating a MUST-FIX (an information leak, lost observability) from a mere style preference matters because the error envelope is a consistency contract every consumer of the API relies on, not a matter of per-handler taste.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix can be verified with a test that throws a domain error inside the service, calls the endpoint, asserts the response matches the centralized handler's standard envelope ({statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}), and asserts the body contains none of the original internal driver or ORM message text.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["centralized error handling","error envelope","hide by default","expected vs unexpected errors","observability"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"So the try/catch in every handler duplicates what the framework's exception filter already does — that's the whole point of centralizing error handling into one response shape. Right now every handler formats errors differently, so clients can't rely on one contract. We're also leaking the raw exception message, which can carry DB or ORM internals that should never reach the client. Expected cases like not-found get caught locally and collapse into a generic response instead of a real 404, and swallowing errors without logging kills observability when something actually breaks. The fix: remove the try/catch, throw typed exceptions for expected cases, let everything else bubble to the central handler. I'd verify with a test asserting the standard envelope and no leaked internals.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The try/catch in each handler is duplicating the framework's centralized error layer — that's literally what an exception filter is for. Since every handler formats its own response, the shape drifts across endpoints and clients can't parse one contract. We're also returning the raw exception message, which can leak internal driver or ORM details that should stay server-side. On top of that, an expected case like not-found gets caught locally and folded into the same generic response as everything else, so it never becomes a real 404. And when errors are swallowed without logging, there's nothing to grep for once something actually breaks in production. To confirm any change here, I'd throw a domain error in the service and check the response against the expected envelope, with no internal message text showing up.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the main problem is that these handlers are each rolling their own try/catch, which just duplicates what the centralized error handling in the framework already does. And because of that, we're passing the raw exception message back to the caller — that could easily include internal database or ORM details that shouldn't leave the server. Also, something like a not-found case gets treated the same as any other failure since it's caught locally, so it never actually turns into a proper 404 status, it just becomes another generic error.","sonnet":48,"haiku":48},{"level":4,"answer":"I guess the main thing I'd flag is that when something like a not-found case happens, it's getting handled the same way as any other error, so it doesn't come back as its own distinct status — it just gets grouped in with everything else.","sonnet":16,"haiku":16},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I'd probably just add more try/catch blocks around the database calls to make sure nothing crashes the server, and maybe wrap the whole thing in a global try/catch at the top level too so errors don't take down the app. I think the main goal is just making sure the server doesn't crash, so as long as it stays up and returns some response, that should be fine for production.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[technical] Names the real defect: a per-handler `try/catch` completely bypasses the framework's centralized error-handling layer (an exception filter on Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, or a central middleware + `recover()` on Go) — the result is every handler deciding its own error shape, when the whole reason a central layer exists is to define ONE envelope for the entire system.","[technical] Points out that returning an exception's raw message straight to the client violates hide-by-default: the raw message of whatever exception was thrown (a DB driver, an ORM...) can carry table names, column names, query details — details that must never reach the client.","[problemSolving] Spots the expected-vs-unexpected conflation: an expected domain \"not found\" error is reported as a generic error instead of a distinct \"not found\" status/shape; and because nothing is logged, when a genuinely unexpected error happens in production there's no trace left to investigate — observability is effectively gone.","[problemSolving] Proposes the correct fix: remove the local `try/catch` from the handlers entirely, throw a typed error for expected cases — a typed exception class on Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, or a typed sentinel error value on Go — and let everything else fall through naturally to the ONE centralized error-handling point that already exists exactly for this.","[communication] Delivers the review constructively: separates what's a MUST-FIX (information leak, lost observability) from what's mere style; explains WHY — the envelope is a consistency contract for every caller of the API, not a matter of personal taste per handler.","[testing] Proves it with a concrete test: throw a domain error inside the service, assert the response matches the centralized handler's standard envelope shape, and assert the body contains NONE of the original internal message text (e.g. a raw DB driver message)."],"checklist":[{"text":"A per-handler try/catch that builds its own JSON error response duplicates work already owned by the framework's centralized error-handling layer (an exception filter on Nest/Spring/ASP.NET, or a central middleware plus recover() on Go) — the entire reason that layer exists is to define ONE response envelope for the whole system.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"When every handler catches its own errors, the response shape drifts across endpoints — one returns {message}, another returns {error, detail} — forcing clients to guess the format per endpoint instead of parsing a single contract.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Returning an exception's raw message straight to the client violates hide-by-default: that message belongs to whatever exception fired underneath — a DB driver, an ORM — and can carry table names, column names, or query fragments that must never reach outside the server.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Catching an expected domain error such as \"not found\" inside a local try/catch collapses it into the same generic response as any other failure, throwing away the information that would let it surface as a real 404 instead of a 500.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Swallowing an error without logging it removes any trace of the failure; when a genuinely unexpected error hits production, there is nothing left to grep for, so observability is effectively gone.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The correct fix is to strip the local try/catch out of the handlers entirely: throw a typed exception (NotFoundException, BadRequestException, or an equivalent typed error/sentinel per stack) for expected cases with a message the domain deliberately chose to expose, and let everything else fall through to the one centralized handler that already classifies, logs, and formats it.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Separating a MUST-FIX (an information leak, lost observability) from a mere style preference matters because the error envelope is a consistency contract every consumer of the API relies on, not a matter of per-handler taste.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix can be verified with a test that throws a domain error inside the service, calls the endpoint, asserts the response matches the centralized handler's standard envelope ({statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}), and asserts the body contains none of the original internal driver or ORM message text.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["centralized error handling","error envelope","hide by default","expected vs unexpected errors","observability"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"So the try/catch in every handler duplicates what the framework's exception filter already does — that's the whole point of centralizing error handling into one response shape. Right now every handler formats errors differently, so clients can't rely on one contract. We're also leaking the raw exception message, which can carry DB or ORM internals that should never reach the client. Expected cases like not-found get caught locally and collapse into a generic response instead of a real 404, and swallowing errors without logging kills observability when something actually breaks. The fix: remove the try/catch, throw typed exceptions for expected cases, let everything else bubble to the central handler. I'd verify with a test asserting the standard envelope and no leaked internals.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The try/catch in each handler is duplicating the framework's centralized error layer — that's literally what an exception filter is for. Since every handler formats its own response, the shape drifts across endpoints and clients can't parse one contract. We're also returning the raw exception message, which can leak internal driver or ORM details that should stay server-side. On top of that, an expected case like not-found gets caught locally and folded into the same generic response as everything else, so it never becomes a real 404. And when errors are swallowed without logging, there's nothing to grep for once something actually breaks in production. To confirm any change here, I'd throw a domain error in the service and check the response against the expected envelope, with no internal message text showing up.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the main problem is that these handlers are each rolling their own try/catch, which just duplicates what the centralized error handling in the framework already does. And because of that, we're passing the raw exception message back to the caller — that could easily include internal database or ORM details that shouldn't leave the server. Also, something like a not-found case gets treated the same as any other failure since it's caught locally, so it never actually turns into a proper 404 status, it just becomes another generic error.","sonnet":48,"haiku":48},{"level":4,"answer":"I guess the main thing I'd flag is that when something like a not-found case happens, it's getting handled the same way as any other error, so it doesn't come back as its own distinct status — it just gets grouped in with everything else.","sonnet":16,"haiku":16},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I'd probably just add more try/catch blocks around the database calls to make sure nothing crashes the server, and maybe wrap the whole thing in a global try/catch at the top level too so errors don't take down the app. I think the main goal is just making sure the server doesn't crash, so as long as it stays up and returns some response, that should be fine for production.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nVấn đề không nằm ở việc dùng `try/catch` — mà ở chỗ mỗi handler tự làm luôn công việc vốn thuộc về tầng xử lý lỗi tập trung. Đề xuất: xoá hết `try/catch` cục bộ trong controller, ném `HttpException` có kiểu (`NotFoundException`, `BadRequestException`...) cho những case đoán-trước-được, còn lại để rơi tự nhiên xuống exception filter toàn cục — filter đó đã được đăng ký sẵn đúng cho việc này.\n\n:::muted\nVì sao vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần\n:::\nBa thứ vỡ cùng lúc. Một, envelope trôi dạt: hai handler cho hai shape khác nhau (`{message}` với `{error, detail}`), càng nhiều handler tự viết catch, càng nhiều shape — client phải đoán format theo từng endpoint thay vì parse một contract duy nhất. Hai, hide-by-default bị phá: `err.message` là message gốc của bất kỳ exception nào bên dưới, kể cả lỗi driver DB — trả thẳng ra nghĩa là bất cứ lúc nào tầng dưới đổi thông báo lỗi (đổi ORM, đổi driver), nội bộ hệ thống liền lộ ra ngoài mà không ai chủ ý. Ba, expected và unexpected bị trộn làm một: một domain \"not found\" đáng lẽ 404 lại thành 500 chỉ vì bị `catch` chặn ngang, còn lỗi thật sự bất ngờ thì im lặng biến mất vì không log — khi incident thật xảy ra trên production, không còn dòng log nào để lần theo.\n\n:::muted\nĐề xuất & minh chứng\n:::\nSửa bằng cách gỡ toàn bộ `try/catch` ở handler, để service ném lỗi tự nhiên: case đoán-trước-được ném `HttpException` có kiểu với message do domain chủ động viết (an toàn để lộ), case còn lại cứ để bay thẳng tới filter toàn cục — filter tự lo phân loại, ẩn message thật, log stack trace. Chứng minh bằng một test: cho service ném một domain error, gọi endpoint, assert response khớp đúng envelope chuẩn `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` và assert body không hề chứa message driver/ORM gốc nào.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe problem isn't using `try/catch` — it's that every handler is doing the job that belongs to the centralized error-handling layer. Fix: remove the local `try/catch` from the controller entirely, throw typed `HttpException`s (`NotFoundException`, `BadRequestException`...) for expected cases, and let everything else fall through naturally to the global exception filter — which is already registered exactly for this.\n\n:::muted\nWhy it breaks as the system grows\n:::\nThree things break at once. First, envelope drift: two handlers already produce two different shapes (`{message}` vs `{error, detail}`) — the more handlers write their own catch, the more shapes accumulate, forcing clients to guess the format per endpoint instead of parsing one contract. Second, hide-by-default is broken: `err.message` is the raw message of whatever exception happened underneath, including DB driver errors — returning it as-is means any time an internal detail changes (a different ORM, a different driver), internal system details leak out unintentionally. Third, expected and unexpected errors get merged into one: a domain \"not found\" that should be 404 becomes 500 just because `catch` intercepted it, while a genuinely unexpected error silently vanishes because nothing is logged — when a real incident hits production, there's no log line left to trace it.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & proof\n:::\nFix it by stripping every `try/catch` out of the handlers and letting the service throw naturally: expected cases throw a typed `HttpException` with a message the domain deliberately wrote (safe to expose); everything else flies straight to the global filter, which classifies it, hides the real message, and logs the stack trace. Prove it with a test: make the service throw a domain error, call the endpoint, assert the response matches the standard envelope `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}`, and assert the body contains none of the original driver/ORM message text."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nVấn đề không nằm ở việc dùng `try/catch` — mà ở chỗ mỗi handler tự làm luôn công việc vốn thuộc về tầng xử lý lỗi tập trung. Đề xuất: xoá hết `try/catch` cục bộ trong controller, ném exception có kiểu cho case đoán-trước-được (một domain exception như `OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException`), còn lại để rơi tự nhiên xuống MỘT `@RestControllerAdvice` toàn cục — advice đó tồn tại sẵn đúng cho việc này.\n\n:::muted\nVì sao vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần\n:::\nBa thứ vỡ cùng lúc. Một, envelope trôi dạt: hai handler đã cho hai shape khác nhau (`{message}` với status 500 vs `{error, detail}` với status 400) — càng nhiều handler tự viết catch, càng nhiều shape tích luỹ, buộc client phải đoán format theo từng endpoint thay vì parse một contract duy nhất. Hai, hide-by-default bị phá: `ex.getMessage()` là message gốc của bất kỳ exception nào bên dưới — kể cả lỗi từ driver DB hay JPA/Hibernate — trả thẳng ra client nghĩa là bất cứ lúc nào tầng dưới đổi thông báo lỗi, chi tiết nội bộ (tên bảng, tên cột, chi tiết query) liền lộ ra ngoài mà không ai chủ ý. Ba, expected và unexpected bị trộn làm một: `catch (Exception ex)` quá rộng nên một domain \"not found\" đáng lẽ 404 lại thành 500, còn một lỗi thật sự bất ngờ (NPE, mất kết nối DB) thì im lặng biến mất vì không có dòng log nào — khi incident thật xảy ra trên production, không còn dấu vết nào để lần theo.\n\n:::muted\nĐề xuất & minh chứng\n:::\nSửa bằng cách gỡ toàn bộ `try/catch` ở handler, để service ném lỗi tự nhiên: thêm class `OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException` mang message an toàn do domain chủ động viết, rồi đăng ký một `GlobalExceptionHandler` dùng `@RestControllerAdvice` với các `@ExceptionHandler` cụ thể (khớp `OrderNotFoundException` trước) cộng một `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` làm catch-net cuối cùng — nhánh này thay message thật bằng một message chung chung, `log.error(...)` đầy đủ stack trace, và mọi nhánh đều build ra CÙNG một `ErrorEnvelope {statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` duy nhất. Chứng minh bằng một test: cho service ném `OrderNotFoundException`, gọi endpoint, assert response khớp đúng envelope chuẩn đó với status 404, và assert body không hề chứa message driver/JPA gốc nào.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe problem isn't using `try/catch` — it's that every handler is doing the job that belongs to the centralized error-handling layer. Fix: remove the local `try/catch` from the controller entirely, throw a typed exception for expected cases (a domain exception like `OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException`), and let everything else fall through naturally to ONE global `@RestControllerAdvice` — that advice already exists exactly for this.\n\n:::muted\nWhy it breaks as the system grows\n:::\nThree things break at once. First, envelope drift: two handlers already produce two different shapes (`{message}` with status 500 vs `{error, detail}` with status 400) — the more handlers write their own catch, the more shapes accumulate, forcing clients to guess the format per endpoint instead of parsing one contract. Second, hide-by-default is broken: `ex.getMessage()` is the raw message of whatever exception happened underneath — including a DB driver or JPA/Hibernate error — returning it as-is means any time an internal detail changes, internal system details (table names, column names, query details) leak out unintentionally. Third, expected and unexpected errors get merged into one: `catch (Exception ex)` is far too broad, so a domain \"not found\" that should be 404 becomes 500, while a genuinely unexpected error (an NPE, a dropped DB connection) silently vanishes because nothing is logged — when a real incident hits production, there's no log line left to trace it.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & proof\n:::\nFix it by stripping every `try/catch` out of the handlers and letting the service throw naturally: add an `OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException` class carrying a safe message the domain deliberately wrote, then register a `GlobalExceptionHandler` using `@RestControllerAdvice` with specific `@ExceptionHandler`s (matching `OrderNotFoundException` first) plus an `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` as the final catch-net — that branch replaces the real message with a generic one, `log.error(...)`s the full stack trace, and every branch builds the SAME single `ErrorEnvelope {statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}`. Prove it with a test: make the service throw `OrderNotFoundException`, call the endpoint, assert the response matches that standard envelope with status 404, and assert the body contains none of the original driver/JPA message text."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nVấn đề không nằm ở việc dùng `try/catch` — mà ở chỗ mỗi endpoint tự làm luôn công việc vốn thuộc về tầng xử lý lỗi tập trung. Đề xuất: xoá hết `try/catch` cục bộ trong từng endpoint, ném exception có kiểu domain tự viết (vd `ItemNotFoundException`, `ValidationException`) cho những case đoán-trước-được, còn lại để rơi tự nhiên xuống một `ExceptionHandlingMiddleware` đăng ký ở đầu pipeline (`app.UseMiddleware<ExceptionHandlingMiddleware>()`) — middleware đó đã tồn tại sẵn đúng cho việc này.\n\n:::muted\nVì sao vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần\n:::\nBa thứ vỡ cùng lúc. Một, envelope trôi dạt: hai endpoint cho hai anonymous object khác nhau (`{message}` với `{error, detail}`), càng nhiều endpoint tự viết catch, càng nhiều shape — client phải đoán format theo từng route thay vì parse một contract duy nhất. Hai, hide-by-default bị phá: `ex.Message` là message gốc của bất kỳ exception nào bên dưới, kể cả lỗi driver EF Core/SQL — trả thẳng ra nghĩa là bất cứ lúc nào tầng dưới đổi thông báo lỗi (đổi ORM, đổi connection string), nội bộ hệ thống liền lộ ra ngoài mà không ai chủ ý. Ba, expected và unexpected bị trộn làm một: một domain \"not found\" đáng lẽ 404 lại thành 500 chỉ vì bị `catch` chặn ngang, còn lỗi thật sự bất ngờ thì im lặng biến mất vì không có `_logger.LogError` nào — khi incident thật xảy ra trên production, không còn dòng log nào để lần theo.\n\n:::muted\nĐề xuất & minh chứng\n:::\nSửa bằng cách gỡ toàn bộ `try/catch` ở từng endpoint, để service ném lỗi tự nhiên: case đoán-trước-được ném một exception có kiểu domain với message do domain chủ động viết (an toàn để lộ), case còn lại cứ để bay thẳng tới `ExceptionHandlingMiddleware` — middleware tự lo `switch` phân loại theo kiểu exception, ẩn message thật sau `\"Internal server error\"` cho nhánh 500, và chỉ ở nhánh đó mới gọi `_logger.LogError(ex, ...)` với stack đầy đủ. Chứng minh bằng một test: cho service ném một domain exception, gọi endpoint, assert response khớp đúng envelope chuẩn `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` và assert body không hề chứa message driver EF Core/SQL gốc nào.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe problem isn't using `try/catch` — it's that every endpoint is doing the job that belongs to the centralized error-handling layer. Fix: remove the local `try/catch` from every endpoint, throw a typed domain exception (e.g. `ItemNotFoundException`, `ValidationException`) for expected cases, and let everything else fall through naturally to an `ExceptionHandlingMiddleware` registered at the head of the pipeline (`app.UseMiddleware<ExceptionHandlingMiddleware>()`) — which already exists exactly for this.\n\n:::muted\nWhy it breaks as the system grows\n:::\nThree things break at once. First, envelope drift: two endpoints already produce two different anonymous objects (`{message}` vs `{error, detail}`) — the more endpoints write their own catch, the more shapes accumulate, forcing clients to guess the format per route instead of parsing one contract. Second, hide-by-default is broken: `ex.Message` is the raw message of whatever exception happened underneath, including an EF Core/SQL driver error — returning it as-is means any time an internal detail changes (a different ORM, a different connection string), internal system details leak out unintentionally. Third, expected and unexpected errors get merged into one: a domain \"not found\" that should be 404 becomes 500 just because `catch` intercepted it, while a genuinely unexpected error silently vanishes because there's no `_logger.LogError` anywhere — when a real incident hits production, there's no log line left to trace it.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & proof\n:::\nFix it by stripping every `try/catch` out of the endpoints and letting the service throw naturally: expected cases throw a typed domain exception with a message the domain deliberately wrote (safe to expose); everything else flies straight to `ExceptionHandlingMiddleware`, which `switch`es on exception type, hides the real message behind `\"Internal server error\"` for the 500 branch, and only there calls `_logger.LogError(ex, ...)` with the full stack. Prove it with a test: make the service throw a domain exception, call the endpoint, assert the response matches the standard envelope `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}`, and assert the body contains none of the original EF Core/SQL driver message text."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nVấn đề không nằm ở việc kiểm tra `err != nil` — mà ở chỗ mỗi handler tự làm luôn công việc vốn thuộc về middleware `ErrorHandler` tập trung. Đề xuất: gỡ hết đoạn `c.JSON(...)` build envelope thủ công trong từng handler, thay bằng gọi `c.Error(err); return` để đẩy lỗi vào `c.Errors`, và để middleware `ErrorHandler()` đăng ký ở đầu chain (`r.Use(...)`) đọc lại, phân loại rồi trả đúng một envelope duy nhất — kể cả `panic` cũng đã có `recover()` lo, không cần handler tự bọc gì thêm.\n\n:::muted\nVì sao vỡ khi hệ thống lớn dần\n:::\nBa thứ vỡ cùng lúc. Một, envelope trôi dạt: hai handler cho hai shape khác nhau (`{message}` với `{error, detail}`), càng nhiều handler tự viết `if err != nil { c.JSON(...) }`, càng nhiều shape — client phải đoán format theo từng endpoint thay vì parse một contract duy nhất. Hai, hide-by-default bị phá: `err.Error()` là message gốc của bất kỳ lỗi nào bên dưới, kể cả lỗi driver DB hay ORM — trả thẳng ra nghĩa là bất cứ lúc nào tầng dưới đổi thông báo lỗi, nội bộ hệ thống liền lộ ra ngoài mà không ai chủ ý. Ba, expected và unexpected bị trộn làm một: một domain \"not found\" đáng lẽ 404 lại bị handler tự gán 500 vì code chỉ có mỗi một nhánh xử lý lỗi, còn lỗi thật sự bất ngờ (một `panic` ở tầng dưới, một driver bị rớt kết nối) thì không đi qua `recover()` nào theo đường này và cũng chẳng có `log.Printf` nào ghi lại — khi incident thật xảy ra trên production, không còn dòng log nào để lần theo.\n\n:::muted\nĐề xuất & minh chứng\n:::\nSửa bằng cách gỡ toàn bộ khối build-response-thủ-công ở handler, chỉ giữ lại `c.Error(err); return` cho mọi lỗi nghiệp vụ: định nghĩa sentinel error kiểu (`ErrNotFound`, `ErrValidation`) cho các case đoán-trước-được với message do domain chủ động viết (an toàn để lộ), middleware `ErrorHandler()` đọc `c.Errors.Last().Err`, dùng `errors.Is(...)` để phân loại rồi gọi đúng một hàm `writeEnvelope(...)` — còn `panic` thì `defer recover()` trong chính middleware đó bắt lại, log `debug.Stack()` nội bộ và trả về message chung. Chứng minh bằng một test: cho service trả về `ErrNotFound`, gọi endpoint, assert response khớp đúng envelope chuẩn `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` và assert body không hề chứa message driver/ORM gốc nào.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe problem isn't checking `err != nil` — it's that every handler is doing the job that belongs to the central `ErrorHandler` middleware. Fix: strip out the hand-built `c.JSON(...)` envelope from every handler, replace it with `c.Error(err); return` to push the error onto `c.Errors`, and let the `ErrorHandler()` middleware registered at the head of the chain (`r.Use(...)`) read it back, classify it, and return exactly one envelope — even `panic` is already covered by its `recover()`, no handler needs to wrap anything extra.\n\n:::muted\nWhy it breaks as the system grows\n:::\nThree things break at once. First, envelope drift: two handlers already produce two different shapes (`{message}` vs `{error, detail}`) — the more handlers write their own `if err != nil { c.JSON(...) }`, the more shapes accumulate, forcing clients to guess the format per endpoint instead of parsing one contract. Second, hide-by-default is broken: `err.Error()` is the raw message of whatever error happened underneath, including a DB driver or ORM error — returning it as-is means any time an internal detail changes, it leaks out unintentionally. Third, expected and unexpected errors get merged into one: a domain \"not found\" that should be 404 ends up hard-coded to 500 because the handler only has one error branch, while a genuinely unexpected error (a `panic` from a lower layer, a dropped DB connection) never reaches any `recover()` on this path and nothing calls `log.Printf` either — when a real incident hits production, there's no log line left to trace it.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & proof\n:::\nFix it by stripping the hand-built response block out of every handler, keeping only `c.Error(err); return` for every business error: declare typed sentinel errors (`ErrNotFound`, `ErrValidation`) for the expected cases with a message the domain deliberately wrote (safe to expose), let the `ErrorHandler()` middleware read `c.Errors.Last().Err`, classify it with `errors.Is(...)`, and call exactly one `writeEnvelope(...)`; a `panic` is caught by that same middleware's `defer recover()`, logs `debug.Stack()` internally, and returns the generic message. Prove it with a test: make the service return `ErrNotFound`, call the endpoint, assert the response matches the standard envelope `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}`, and assert the body contains none of the original driver/ORM message text."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation', '231d8cc57-stem1', '0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation/11', 11, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'optimize', ARRAY['logging-performance', 'hot-path', 'transport-cost']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Ví dụ minh hoạ qua TypeScript/NestJS. Logging ở đây ĐÚNG, không có gì hỏng cả, chỉ là đắt. Nhìn config bootstrap logger dưới đây, hãy trình bày cách bạn tiếp cận việc tối ưu: bạn đo gì đầu tiên, bạn thay đổi gì, và mỗi thay đổi đánh đổi ra sao về khả năng quan sát (observability).","givenCode":"```typescript\n// logger.config.ts\nexport function getWinstonOptions(): WinstonModuleOptions {\n    const prettyFormat = winston.format.combine(\n        winston.format.colorize(),\n        winston.format.timestamp(),\n        nestWinstonModuleUtilities.format.nestLike(\"api\"),\n    )   // ← colorized/pretty, không check NODE_ENV — cùng một format chạy cả ở production\n\n    return {\n        transports: [\n            new winston.transports.Console({ format: prettyFormat }),\n            new SyncFileTransport(\"logs/app.log\", { format: prettyFormat }),\n        ],\n    }\n}\n\n// sync-file.transport.ts\nclass SyncFileTransport extends winston.Transport {\n    constructor(private readonly filename: string, opts: winston.TransportStreamOptions) {\n        super(opts)\n    }\n    log(info: Record<string, unknown>, callback: () => void): void {\n        fs.appendFileSync(this.filename, JSON.stringify(info) + \"\\n\")   // ← ghi đồng bộ, chạy ở MỌI lần log\n        callback()\n    }\n}\n\n// request-logging.interceptor.ts\n@Injectable()\nexport class RequestLoggingInterceptor implements NestInterceptor {\n    constructor(private readonly logger: LoggerService) {}\n\n    intercept(context: ExecutionContext, next: CallHandler): Observable<unknown> {\n        const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest<Request>()\n        this.logger.log(\"incoming request\", { body: req.body })   // ← full request body, mọi request, level info\n\n        return next.handle().pipe(\n            tap((responseBody) => this.logger.log(\"outgoing response\", { body: responseBody })),   // ← full response body, mọi request, level info\n        )\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated via TypeScript/NestJS. The logging here is CORRECT, nothing is broken, it's just expensive. Given the logger bootstrap config below, talk through how you'd approach optimizing it: what you measure first, what you change, and what trade-off each change costs in observability.","givenCode":"```typescript\n// logger.config.ts\nexport function getWinstonOptions(): WinstonModuleOptions {\n    const prettyFormat = winston.format.combine(\n        winston.format.colorize(),\n        winston.format.timestamp(),\n        nestWinstonModuleUtilities.format.nestLike(\"api\"),\n    )   // ← colorized/pretty, no NODE_ENV check — the same format ships to production\n\n    return {\n        transports: [\n            new winston.transports.Console({ format: prettyFormat }),\n            new SyncFileTransport(\"logs/app.log\", { format: prettyFormat }),\n        ],\n    }\n}\n\n// sync-file.transport.ts\nclass SyncFileTransport extends winston.Transport {\n    constructor(private readonly filename: string, opts: winston.TransportStreamOptions) {\n        super(opts)\n    }\n    log(info: Record<string, unknown>, callback: () => void): void {\n        fs.appendFileSync(this.filename, JSON.stringify(info) + \"\\n\")   // ← synchronous write, runs on EVERY log call\n        callback()\n    }\n}\n\n// request-logging.interceptor.ts\n@Injectable()\nexport class RequestLoggingInterceptor implements NestInterceptor {\n    constructor(private readonly logger: LoggerService) {}\n\n    intercept(context: ExecutionContext, next: CallHandler): Observable<unknown> {\n        const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest<Request>()\n        this.logger.log(\"incoming request\", { body: req.body })   // ← full request body, every request, info level\n\n        return next.handle().pipe(\n            tap((responseBody) => this.logger.log(\"outgoing response\", { body: responseBody })),   // ← full response body, every request, info level\n        )\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh họa bằng java. Logging ở đây ĐÚNG, không có gì hỏng cả, chỉ là đắt. Nhìn config bootstrap logger dưới đây, hãy trình bày cách bạn tiếp cận việc tối ưu: bạn đo gì đầu tiên, bạn thay đổi gì, và mỗi thay đổi đánh đổi ra sao về khả năng quan sát (observability).","givenCode":"```java\n// LogbackConfig.java — cấu hình Logback bằng code, chạy trên MỌI môi trường (không guard theo active profile)\n@Configuration\npublic class LogbackConfig {\n\n    @PostConstruct\n    public void configureAppenders() {\n        LoggerContext context = (LoggerContext) LoggerFactory.getILoggerFactory();\n\n        PatternLayoutEncoder prettyEncoder = new PatternLayoutEncoder();\n        prettyEncoder.setContext(context);\n        prettyEncoder.setPattern(\"%highlight(%-5level) %cyan(%logger{36}) - %msg%n\");   // ← mã màu ANSI gắn cứng vào pattern, không có guard springProfile(\"dev\")\n        prettyEncoder.start();\n\n        ConsoleAppender<ILoggingEvent> console = new ConsoleAppender<>();\n        console.setContext(context);\n        console.setEncoder(prettyEncoder);\n        console.start();\n\n        SyncFileAppender file = new SyncFileAppender(\"logs/app.log\", prettyEncoder);   // ← DÙNG LẠI đúng encoder có màu cho sink file\n        file.setContext(context);\n        file.start();\n\n        Logger root = context.getLogger(Logger.ROOT_LOGGER_NAME);\n        root.addAppender(console);\n        root.addAppender(file);\n    }\n}\n\n// SyncFileAppender.java\npublic class SyncFileAppender extends AppenderBase<ILoggingEvent> {\n    private final String filename;\n    private final Encoder<ILoggingEvent> encoder;\n    private Writer writer;\n\n    public SyncFileAppender(String filename, Encoder<ILoggingEvent> encoder) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n        this.encoder = encoder;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void start() {\n        try {\n            this.writer = new FileWriter(filename, true);\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n        }\n        super.start();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void append(ILoggingEvent event) {\n        try {\n            writer.write(new String(encoder.encode(event)));\n            writer.flush();   // ← ghi đồng bộ + flush trên thread gọi, ở MỌI lần log\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            addError(\"failed to write log line\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// RequestLoggingFilter.java\n@Component\npublic class RequestLoggingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RequestLoggingFilter.class);\n\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)\n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        ContentCachingRequestWrapper wrappedReq = new ContentCachingRequestWrapper(req);\n        ContentCachingResponseWrapper wrappedRes = new ContentCachingResponseWrapper(res);\n\n        chain.doFilter(wrappedReq, wrappedRes);\n\n        log.info(\"incoming request body={}\", new String(wrappedReq.getContentAsByteArray()));    // ← full request body, mọi request, level info\n        log.info(\"outgoing response body={}\", new String(wrappedRes.getContentAsByteArray()));    // ← full response body, mọi request, level info\n        wrappedRes.copyBodyToResponse();\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. The logging here is CORRECT, nothing is broken, it's just expensive. Given the logger bootstrap config below, talk through how you'd approach optimizing it: what you measure first, what you change, and what trade-off each change costs in observability.","givenCode":"```java\n// LogbackConfig.java — programmatic Logback setup, runs on EVERY environment (no active-profile guard)\n@Configuration\npublic class LogbackConfig {\n\n    @PostConstruct\n    public void configureAppenders() {\n        LoggerContext context = (LoggerContext) LoggerFactory.getILoggerFactory();\n\n        PatternLayoutEncoder prettyEncoder = new PatternLayoutEncoder();\n        prettyEncoder.setContext(context);\n        prettyEncoder.setPattern(\"%highlight(%-5level) %cyan(%logger{36}) - %msg%n\");   // ← ANSI color codes baked into the pattern, no springProfile(\"dev\") guard\n        prettyEncoder.start();\n\n        ConsoleAppender<ILoggingEvent> console = new ConsoleAppender<>();\n        console.setContext(context);\n        console.setEncoder(prettyEncoder);\n        console.start();\n\n        SyncFileAppender file = new SyncFileAppender(\"logs/app.log\", prettyEncoder);   // ← SAME colored encoder reused on the file sink\n        file.setContext(context);\n        file.start();\n\n        Logger root = context.getLogger(Logger.ROOT_LOGGER_NAME);\n        root.addAppender(console);\n        root.addAppender(file);\n    }\n}\n\n// SyncFileAppender.java\npublic class SyncFileAppender extends AppenderBase<ILoggingEvent> {\n    private final String filename;\n    private final Encoder<ILoggingEvent> encoder;\n    private Writer writer;\n\n    public SyncFileAppender(String filename, Encoder<ILoggingEvent> encoder) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n        this.encoder = encoder;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void start() {\n        try {\n            this.writer = new FileWriter(filename, true);\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n        }\n        super.start();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void append(ILoggingEvent event) {\n        try {\n            writer.write(new String(encoder.encode(event)));\n            writer.flush();   // ← synchronous write + flush on the calling thread, EVERY log call\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            addError(\"failed to write log line\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// RequestLoggingFilter.java\n@Component\npublic class RequestLoggingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RequestLoggingFilter.class);\n\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)\n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        ContentCachingRequestWrapper wrappedReq = new ContentCachingRequestWrapper(req);\n        ContentCachingResponseWrapper wrappedRes = new ContentCachingResponseWrapper(res);\n\n        chain.doFilter(wrappedReq, wrappedRes);\n\n        log.info(\"incoming request body={}\", new String(wrappedReq.getContentAsByteArray()));    // ← full request body, every request, info level\n        log.info(\"outgoing response body={}\", new String(wrappedRes.getContentAsByteArray()));    // ← full response body, every request, info level\n        wrappedRes.copyBodyToResponse();\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh họa bằng C#. Logging ở đây ĐÚNG, không có gì hỏng cả, chỉ là đắt. Nhìn config bootstrap logger dưới đây, hãy trình bày cách bạn tiếp cận việc tối ưu: bạn đo gì đầu tiên, bạn thay đổi gì, và mỗi thay đổi đánh đổi ra sao về khả năng quan sát (observability).","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Program.cs\nvar builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);\n\nvar theme = AnsiConsoleTheme.Code;   // ← theme tô màu, không check environment — theme này bị tái dùng cho cả file sink bên dưới\n\nbuilder.Host.UseSerilog((ctx, cfg) => cfg\n    .Enrich.FromLogContext()\n    .WriteTo.Console(theme: theme)\n    .WriteTo.Sink(new SyncFileSink(\"logs/app.log\", theme)));   // ← ép theme tô màu của console vào luôn file sink\n\nvar app = builder.Build();\napp.UseMiddleware<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware>();\n\n// SyncFileSink.cs\npublic sealed class SyncFileSink : ILogEventSink\n{\n    private readonly string _path;\n    private readonly ConsoleTheme _theme;\n    private readonly object _gate = new();\n\n    public SyncFileSink(string path, ConsoleTheme theme)\n    {\n        _path = path;\n        _theme = theme;\n    }\n\n    public void Emit(LogEvent logEvent)\n    {\n        var writer = new StringWriter();\n        new ThemedMessageTemplateTextFormatter(_theme, logEvent.MessageTemplate, null).Format(logEvent, writer);   // ← render cả mã ANSI màu vào text, dù file thì không ai \"xem\" màu\n\n        lock (_gate)   // mọi request tranh nhau một file handle\n        {\n            File.AppendAllText(_path, writer.ToString() + Environment.NewLine);   // ← ghi đĩa đồng bộ, chạy ở MỌI lần log, ngay trên thread đang xử lý request\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware.cs\npublic sealed class RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware\n{\n    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;\n    private readonly ILogger<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware> _logger;\n\n    public RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, ILogger<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware> logger)\n    {\n        _next = next;\n        _logger = logger;\n    }\n\n    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)\n    {\n        context.Request.EnableBuffering();\n        using var reader = new StreamReader(context.Request.Body, leaveOpen: true);\n        var requestBody = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();   // đọc và buffer nguyên request body vào memory, mọi request\n        context.Request.Body.Position = 0;\n        _logger.LogInformation(\"incoming request {Body}\", requestBody);   // ← full body, mọi request, level Information\n\n        var originalBody = context.Response.Body;\n        using var buffer = new MemoryStream();\n        context.Response.Body = buffer;\n\n        await _next(context);\n\n        buffer.Position = 0;\n        var responseBody = await new StreamReader(buffer).ReadToEndAsync();   // full response body, mọi request\n        _logger.LogInformation(\"outgoing response {Body}\", responseBody);   // ← full body, mọi request, level Information\n\n        buffer.Position = 0;\n        await buffer.CopyToAsync(originalBody);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#. The logging here is CORRECT, nothing is broken, it's just expensive. Given the logger bootstrap config below, talk through how you'd approach optimizing it: what you measure first, what you change, and what trade-off each change costs in observability.","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Program.cs\nvar builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);\n\nvar theme = AnsiConsoleTheme.Code;   // ← colorized theme, no environment check — the same theme is reused for the file sink below\n\nbuilder.Host.UseSerilog((ctx, cfg) => cfg\n    .Enrich.FromLogContext()\n    .WriteTo.Console(theme: theme)\n    .WriteTo.Sink(new SyncFileSink(\"logs/app.log\", theme)));   // ← the console's colored theme forced onto the file sink too\n\nvar app = builder.Build();\napp.UseMiddleware<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware>();\n\n// SyncFileSink.cs\npublic sealed class SyncFileSink : ILogEventSink\n{\n    private readonly string _path;\n    private readonly ConsoleTheme _theme;\n    private readonly object _gate = new();\n\n    public SyncFileSink(string path, ConsoleTheme theme)\n    {\n        _path = path;\n        _theme = theme;\n    }\n\n    public void Emit(LogEvent logEvent)\n    {\n        var writer = new StringWriter();\n        new ThemedMessageTemplateTextFormatter(_theme, logEvent.MessageTemplate, null).Format(logEvent, writer);   // ← renders ANSI color codes into the text, though a file is never \"viewed\" in color\n\n        lock (_gate)   // every request fights over one file handle\n        {\n            File.AppendAllText(_path, writer.ToString() + Environment.NewLine);   // ← synchronous disk write, runs on EVERY log call, on the thread handling the request\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware.cs\npublic sealed class RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware\n{\n    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;\n    private readonly ILogger<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware> _logger;\n\n    public RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, ILogger<RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware> logger)\n    {\n        _next = next;\n        _logger = logger;\n    }\n\n    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)\n    {\n        context.Request.EnableBuffering();\n        using var reader = new StreamReader(context.Request.Body, leaveOpen: true);\n        var requestBody = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();   // buffers the whole request body into memory, every request\n        context.Request.Body.Position = 0;\n        _logger.LogInformation(\"incoming request {Body}\", requestBody);   // ← full body, every request, Information level\n\n        var originalBody = context.Response.Body;\n        using var buffer = new MemoryStream();\n        context.Response.Body = buffer;\n\n        await _next(context);\n\n        buffer.Position = 0;\n        var responseBody = await new StreamReader(buffer).ReadToEndAsync();   // full response body, every request\n        _logger.LogInformation(\"outgoing response {Body}\", responseBody);   // ← full body, every request, Information level\n\n        buffer.Position = 0;\n        await buffer.CopyToAsync(originalBody);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng go. Logging ở đây ĐÚNG, không có gì hỏng cả, chỉ là đắt. Nhìn code setup logger dưới đây, hãy trình bày cách bạn tiếp cận việc tối ưu: bạn đo gì đầu tiên, bạn thay đổi gì, và mỗi thay đổi đánh đổi ra sao về khả năng quan sát (observability).","givenCode":"```go\n// logger.go\nfunc newLogger() *zap.Logger {\n    // ← luôn dùng consoleEncoder có màu, hướng tới mắt người — không check env, cùng một config chạy cả production\n    consoleEncoder := zapcore.NewConsoleEncoder(zapcore.EncoderConfig{\n        TimeKey:     \"ts\",\n        LevelKey:    \"level\",\n        MessageKey:  \"msg\",\n        EncodeLevel: zapcore.CapitalColorLevelEncoder,\n        EncodeTime:  zapcore.ISO8601TimeEncoder,\n    })\n\n    core := zapcore.NewTee(\n        zapcore.NewCore(consoleEncoder, zapcore.AddSync(os.Stdout), zapcore.InfoLevel),\n        zapcore.NewCore(consoleEncoder, &syncFileWriter{filename: \"logs/app.log\"}, zapcore.InfoLevel),\n        // ← file core dùng LẠI cùng consoleEncoder có màu — aggregator cuối cùng phải parse ANSI escape code thay vì JSON\n    )\n    return zap.New(core)\n}\n\n// sync_file_writer.go\ntype syncFileWriter struct {\n    filename string\n}\n\n// Write mở, append, rồi đóng file ở MỖI lần gọi — không buffer, không async — chạy ở mọi dòng log\nfunc (w *syncFileWriter) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {\n    f, err := os.OpenFile(w.filename, os.O_CREATE|os.O_APPEND|os.O_WRONLY, 0644)\n    if err != nil {\n        return 0, err\n    }\n    defer f.Close()\n    return f.Write(p)\n}\n\nfunc (w *syncFileWriter) Sync() error {\n    return nil\n}\n\n// request_logging.go\nfunc RequestLoggingMiddleware(logger *zap.Logger) gin.HandlerFunc {\n    return func(c *gin.Context) {\n        bodyBytes, _ := io.ReadAll(c.Request.Body)\n        c.Request.Body = io.NopCloser(bytes.NewBuffer(bodyBytes))\n        logger.Info(\"incoming request\", zap.ByteString(\"body\", bodyBytes)) // ← full request body, mọi request, level info\n\n        bw := &responseBodyWriter{ResponseWriter: c.Writer, body: &bytes.Buffer{}}\n        c.Writer = bw\n\n        c.Next()\n\n        logger.Info(\"outgoing response\", zap.ByteString(\"body\", bw.body.Bytes())) // ← full response body, mọi request, level info\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in go. The logging here is CORRECT, nothing is broken, it's just expensive. Given the logger setup code below, talk through how you'd approach optimizing it: what you measure first, what you change, and what trade-off each change costs in observability.","givenCode":"```go\n// logger.go\nfunc newLogger() *zap.Logger {\n    // ← always the colored, human-oriented console encoder — no env check, the same config ships to production\n    consoleEncoder := zapcore.NewConsoleEncoder(zapcore.EncoderConfig{\n        TimeKey:     \"ts\",\n        LevelKey:    \"level\",\n        MessageKey:  \"msg\",\n        EncodeLevel: zapcore.CapitalColorLevelEncoder,\n        EncodeTime:  zapcore.ISO8601TimeEncoder,\n    })\n\n    core := zapcore.NewTee(\n        zapcore.NewCore(consoleEncoder, zapcore.AddSync(os.Stdout), zapcore.InfoLevel),\n        zapcore.NewCore(consoleEncoder, &syncFileWriter{filename: \"logs/app.log\"}, zapcore.InfoLevel),\n        // ← file core reuses the SAME colored console encoder — the aggregator ends up parsing ANSI escape codes instead of JSON\n    )\n    return zap.New(core)\n}\n\n// sync_file_writer.go\ntype syncFileWriter struct {\n    filename string\n}\n\n// Write opens, appends, and closes the file on EVERY call — no buffering, no async — runs on every log line\nfunc (w *syncFileWriter) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {\n    f, err := os.OpenFile(w.filename, os.O_CREATE|os.O_APPEND|os.O_WRONLY, 0644)\n    if err != nil {\n        return 0, err\n    }\n    defer f.Close()\n    return f.Write(p)\n}\n\nfunc (w *syncFileWriter) Sync() error {\n    return nil\n}\n\n// request_logging.go\nfunc RequestLoggingMiddleware(logger *zap.Logger) gin.HandlerFunc {\n    return func(c *gin.Context) {\n        bodyBytes, _ := io.ReadAll(c.Request.Body)\n        c.Request.Body = io.NopCloser(bytes.NewBuffer(bodyBytes))\n        logger.Info(\"incoming request\", zap.ByteString(\"body\", bodyBytes)) // ← full request body, every request, info level\n\n        bw := &responseBodyWriter{ResponseWriter: c.Writer, body: &bytes.Buffer{}}\n        c.Writer = bw\n\n        c.Next()\n\n        logger.Info(\"outgoing response\", zap.ByteString(\"body\", bw.body.Bytes())) // ← full response body, every request, info level\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Sau khi áp level policy/sampling, cái gì vẫn nên GIỮ full detail ở error level, và cái gì được phép SAMPLE ở info level?","Làm sao bắt được một PR tương lai lỡ đưa lại body logging vào hot path — dùng lint rule, log-volume budget, hay alert trên tốc độ ghi log?"],"hints":["Có ba thứ đắt trong bức tranh này: format hoá, I/O, và serialize body. Đo cách nào để biết chắc cái nào tốn CPU nhất, cái nào tốn thời gian chặn nhất — thay vì đoán?","Ba thứ đắt cụ thể là: một bộ định dạng màu/pretty không phân biệt môi trường, một lần ghi file đồng bộ chạy trên request path, và cả body request/response được log ở level info. Với mỗi thứ, hỏi \"cái gì đo được đúng chi phí riêng của nó, và thay bằng gì thì vẫn giữ được phần thật sự cần cho debug?\""]},"en":{"followUps":["After applying the level policy/sampling, what should still stay at full detail at error level, and what is safe to sample at info level?","How do you catch a future PR that accidentally reintroduces body logging on the hot path — a lint rule, a log-volume budget, or an alert on log-write rate?"],"hints":["There are three expensive things in this picture: formatting, I/O, and body serialization. How do you measure which one actually costs the most CPU, and which one blocks the longest — instead of guessing?","The three concrete costly things are: a colored/pretty formatter that doesn't check the environment, a synchronous file write that runs on the request path, and both request/response bodies logged at info level. For each one, ask: what actually measures its own cost, and what could replace it while still keeping what's really needed for debugging?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Đo trước khi sửa: xác định cost NÀO đang chiếm ưu thế — CPU của việc format hoá (colorize/pretty), thời gian chặn của I/O đồng bộ, hay chi phí serialize body — thay vì đoán mò rồi sửa cả ba cùng lúc.","[technical] Format màu/pretty chỉ dành cho terminal dev; production phải emit dữ liệu có cấu trúc một dòng (JSON), việc \"làm đẹp\" chuyển sang phía người đọc (log viewer/aggregator) thay vì chạy trên hot path của service.","[technical] Một lần ghi file đồng bộ trên request path chặn đơn vị đang xử lý request đó ở MỖI lần log (event loop ở Node, một worker thread trong pool ở Java/ASP.NET, thread của một goroutine ở Go); chuyển sang cơ chế ghi bất đồng bộ/có buffer, hoặc đơn giản hơn là ghi ra stdout để một collector/sidecar hút đi thay vì tự viết file đồng bộ ngay trong request path.","[technical] Kỷ luật payload trên hot path: không serialize toàn bộ request/response body ở level info; dùng level policy (chỉ log full body khi error), sampling (log 1/N request), hoặc field allow-list (chỉ log vài field thật sự cần).","[testing] Xác nhận hiệu quả bằng load test trước/sau, đo lại p99 và CPU, đồng thời canh log volume vẫn nằm trong budget — không cắt quá tay tới mức mất khả năng debug.","[communication] Nói thẳng đánh đổi: mỗi lần cắt bớt là mất một phần chi tiết debug thật, nên giữ chi tiết đầy đủ ở nhánh lỗi (error path) và chỉ cắt ở nhánh happy path."],"checklist":[{"text":"A single log-heavy endpoint can carry three distinct costs at once — CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, and allocation-bound serialization — and each needs to be isolated and measured on its own before any fix is applied, because changing all three together leaves no way to know which one actually mattered.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Colorized, pretty-printed log output (colorize()/nestLike-style formatting) exists for a human reading a terminal during development; in production nobody watches a live terminal, so the service should emit plain single-line structured JSON and push any prettifying work to the reader side — a log viewer or aggregator like Loki/Grafana — instead of spending CPU on it in the request path.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A logging transport that calls a synchronous file-write function (like fs.appendFileSync) on every log call blocks the thread or event loop handling that request until the disk write completes, so a log-heavy endpoint effectively becomes I/O-starved on every request.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"The fix for a synchronous file-write transport is to switch to an async or buffered writer, or more simply to drop the homegrown file transport entirely and write to stdout so a collector or sidecar (such as Fluentd or Vector) can ship the logs — either way the service stops waiting on disk.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Logging the full request and response body at info level on every single request is the largest serialization and payload cost of the three, since most successful requests never need their full body to debug; the fix is a level policy that keeps full-body logging only on the error path, sampling a fraction of normal requests, or an allow-list that logs just the handful of fields that matter.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"After applying fixes, a before/after load test that re-measures p99 latency and CPU is the only way to confirm the change produced a real improvement, and the same check should verify that log volume hasn't been cut so far that it now hurts debugging.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Every reduction in logging detail trades away some real debugging power, so the honest, sustainable split is to keep full detail on the error path — where an investigation is actually needed — and trim only the happy path, rather than cutting evenly everywhere.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11}],"keywords":["structured logging","synchronous I/O blocking","log sampling","field allow-list","p99 latency"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"There are three separate costs bundled together — CPU from formatting, I/O from blocking writes, and allocation from serialization — and each needs isolating before we touch anything. The colorized pretty-print output is meant for a dev terminal; in production we should emit plain JSON and let a log viewer handle prettifying instead of burning CPU on it per request. The transport is also doing a synchronous file write on every call, which blocks the event loop until disk finishes, so I'd switch to an async/buffered writer or just write to stdout for a collector to ship. Logging the full body at info level on every request is the biggest cost, so I'd cut that to error-path-only plus sampling on the happy path. I'd confirm the fix with a before/after load test on p99 and CPU, and make sure I haven't trimmed logging so far it hurts debugging.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"There are really three different costs mixed together here — CPU from formatting, I/O from the blocking write, and allocation from serialization — and I'd want to isolate which one is actually driving the regression before changing anything. The colorized, pretty-printed output is really meant for a human watching a terminal in dev, so in production we shouldn't be spending CPU on that at all — just emit plain JSON and let the viewer handle formatting. The synchronous file write on every log call is also blocking the event loop until the disk write completes, so it's effectively starving every request on I/O. And logging the entire request and response body at info level on every single call is probably the biggest cost of the three, since most requests never need that detail — I'd only log full bodies on the error path and sample the rest. Any fix here is a real trade-off against debugging power, so I'd keep the error path fully detailed and only trim the happy path.","sonnet":78,"haiku":78},{"level":3,"answer":"I think two things stand out. First, the colorized pretty-printed log format is really meant for someone watching a terminal during development, so keeping that in production is wasted CPU on every request. Second, the log transport looks like it's doing a synchronous write to disk on each call, which blocks the request while it waits for that write to finish.","sonnet":34,"haiku":34},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the logging transport might be writing to disk synchronously on each call, which could be slowing things down a bit if there's a lot of log volume.","sonnet":17,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably just turn logging down to error level everywhere to reduce the overhead, or maybe move to a bigger server so it can handle the extra load from all the logging. Scaling up the instance size seems like the simplest fix if performance is the concern.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Measures before changing anything: identifies WHICH cost dominates — the CPU of formatting (colorize/pretty), the blocking time of synchronous I/O, or the cost of serializing the body — instead of guessing and fixing all three at once.","[technical] Colored/pretty formatting is for the dev terminal only; production must emit one-line structured data (JSON), with the \"prettifying\" moved to the reader side (a log viewer/aggregator) instead of running on the service's hot path.","[technical] A synchronous file write on the request path blocks whatever is handling that request on EVERY log call (the event loop on Node, a pooled worker thread on Java/ASP.NET, a goroutine's thread on Go); switch to an async/buffered write mechanism, or more simply write to stdout for a collector/sidecar to pick up instead of a homegrown synchronous file write in the request path.","[technical] Payload discipline on the hot path: don't serialize the full request/response body at info level; use a level policy (log the full body only on error), sampling (log 1/N requests), or a field allow-list (log only the few fields that actually matter).","[testing] Verifies the win with a before/after load test, re-measuring p99 and CPU, while watching that log volume stays within budget — not cutting so hard that you lose the ability to debug.","[communication] States the trade-off honestly: every cut loses some real debugging detail, so keep full detail on the error path and trim only the happy path."],"checklist":[{"text":"A single log-heavy endpoint can carry three distinct costs at once — CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, and allocation-bound serialization — and each needs to be isolated and measured on its own before any fix is applied, because changing all three together leaves no way to know which one actually mattered.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Colorized, pretty-printed log output (colorize()/nestLike-style formatting) exists for a human reading a terminal during development; in production nobody watches a live terminal, so the service should emit plain single-line structured JSON and push any prettifying work to the reader side — a log viewer or aggregator like Loki/Grafana — instead of spending CPU on it in the request path.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A logging transport that calls a synchronous file-write function (like fs.appendFileSync) on every log call blocks the thread or event loop handling that request until the disk write completes, so a log-heavy endpoint effectively becomes I/O-starved on every request.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"The fix for a synchronous file-write transport is to switch to an async or buffered writer, or more simply to drop the homegrown file transport entirely and write to stdout so a collector or sidecar (such as Fluentd or Vector) can ship the logs — either way the service stops waiting on disk.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Logging the full request and response body at info level on every single request is the largest serialization and payload cost of the three, since most successful requests never need their full body to debug; the fix is a level policy that keeps full-body logging only on the error path, sampling a fraction of normal requests, or an allow-list that logs just the handful of fields that matter.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"After applying fixes, a before/after load test that re-measures p99 latency and CPU is the only way to confirm the change produced a real improvement, and the same check should verify that log volume hasn't been cut so far that it now hurts debugging.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Every reduction in logging detail trades away some real debugging power, so the honest, sustainable split is to keep full detail on the error path — where an investigation is actually needed — and trim only the happy path, rather than cutting evenly everywhere.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11}],"keywords":["structured logging","synchronous I/O blocking","log sampling","field allow-list","p99 latency"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"There are three separate costs bundled together — CPU from formatting, I/O from blocking writes, and allocation from serialization — and each needs isolating before we touch anything. The colorized pretty-print output is meant for a dev terminal; in production we should emit plain JSON and let a log viewer handle prettifying instead of burning CPU on it per request. The transport is also doing a synchronous file write on every call, which blocks the event loop until disk finishes, so I'd switch to an async/buffered writer or just write to stdout for a collector to ship. Logging the full body at info level on every request is the biggest cost, so I'd cut that to error-path-only plus sampling on the happy path. I'd confirm the fix with a before/after load test on p99 and CPU, and make sure I haven't trimmed logging so far it hurts debugging.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"There are really three different costs mixed together here — CPU from formatting, I/O from the blocking write, and allocation from serialization — and I'd want to isolate which one is actually driving the regression before changing anything. The colorized, pretty-printed output is really meant for a human watching a terminal in dev, so in production we shouldn't be spending CPU on that at all — just emit plain JSON and let the viewer handle formatting. The synchronous file write on every log call is also blocking the event loop until the disk write completes, so it's effectively starving every request on I/O. And logging the entire request and response body at info level on every single call is probably the biggest cost of the three, since most requests never need that detail — I'd only log full bodies on the error path and sample the rest. Any fix here is a real trade-off against debugging power, so I'd keep the error path fully detailed and only trim the happy path.","sonnet":78,"haiku":78},{"level":3,"answer":"I think two things stand out. First, the colorized pretty-printed log format is really meant for someone watching a terminal during development, so keeping that in production is wasted CPU on every request. Second, the log transport looks like it's doing a synchronous write to disk on each call, which blocks the request while it waits for that write to finish.","sonnet":34,"haiku":34},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the logging transport might be writing to disk synchronously on each call, which could be slowing things down a bit if there's a lot of log volume.","sonnet":17,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably just turn logging down to error level everywhere to reduce the overhead, or maybe move to a bigger server so it can handle the extra load from all the logging. Scaling up the instance size seems like the simplest fix if performance is the concern.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐầu tiên đo trước khi đổi bất cứ gì: cô lập từng cost bằng cách tắt lần lượt pretty format, sync file transport, và body logging rồi đo lại p99/CPU — vì ba cái này có bản chất khác nhau (CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization) nên \"sửa cả ba cùng lúc\" khiến bạn không biết cái nào thực sự đáng công. Sau khi biết cái nào chiếm ưu thế, sửa theo đúng bản chất từng cái. `colorize()`/`nestLike` là để mắt người đọc trên terminal dev — production không có ai ngồi đọc terminal, nên chuyển sang JSON một dòng thuần, việc tô màu/format đẹp để dành cho phía đọc log (log viewer, Loki/Grafana) chứ không tốn CPU ở hot path. `SyncFileTransport` gọi `fs.appendFileSync` là I/O đồng bộ — mỗi lần log request là một lần chặn event loop chờ đĩa, dồn dập ở endpoint log nhiều thì y hệt một node bị nghẽn I/O giả; đổi sang transport async có buffer, hoặc bỏ hẳn việc tự ghi file mà log ra stdout để một collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector) hút đi — service không còn chờ đĩa. Cuối cùng, log cả body request/response ở info trên MỌI request là chi phí serialize + payload lớn nhất trong ba cái: hầu hết request thành công không cần body đầy đủ để debug, nên hạ xuống level policy (chỉ log full body khi có lỗi), sampling (1/N request bình thường), hoặc field allow-list (chỉ vài field nghiệp vụ cần). Sau khi đổi, phải load test lại đo p99/CPU để xác nhận có cải thiện thật, và theo dõi log volume không tụt quá đà. Đánh đổi phải nói thẳng: mỗi lần cắt là mất một phần khả năng debug — cách hợp lý là giữ full detail ở nhánh lỗi (nơi bạn thực sự cần điều tra) và chỉ cắt gọn ở nhánh happy path, chứ không cắt đều tay khắp nơi.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure before changing anything: isolate each cost by turning off pretty formatting, the sync file transport, and body logging one at a time, then re-measure p99/CPU — because the three have a different nature (CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization), so \"fixing all three at once\" leaves you not knowing which one actually mattered. Once you know which dominates, fix each according to its own nature. `colorize()`/`nestLike` exist for a human's eyes on the dev terminal — nobody is watching a terminal in production, so switch to plain one-line JSON there; the pretty-printing work belongs on the reader side (a log viewer, Loki/Grafana), not burning CPU on the hot path. `SyncFileTransport` calling `fs.appendFileSync` is synchronous I/O — every logged request blocks the event loop waiting on disk, and on a log-heavy endpoint that piles up exactly like an I/O-starved node; switch to an async/buffered transport, or better, drop the homegrown file write entirely and log to stdout for a collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector) to pick up — the service no longer waits on disk. Finally, logging both the request and response body at info on EVERY request is the largest serialization + payload cost of the three: most successful requests don't need the full body to debug, so drop it to a level policy (full body only on error), sampling (1/N normal requests), or a field allow-list (only the handful of business fields that matter). After changing anything, load-test again to confirm a real p99/CPU improvement, and watch that log volume hasn't dropped so far it hurts debugging. Be honest about the trade-off: every cut costs some real debugging power — the sane split is full detail on the error path (where you actually need to investigate) and a trimmed happy path, not an even cut everywhere."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐầu tiên đo trước khi đổi bất cứ gì: cô lập từng cost bằng cách tắt lần lượt encoder pattern có màu, `SyncFileAppender`, và việc log body, rồi đo lại p99/CPU — vì ba cái này có bản chất khác nhau (CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization) nên \"sửa cả ba cùng lúc\" khiến bạn không biết cái nào thực sự đáng công. Sau khi biết cái nào chiếm ưu thế, sửa theo đúng bản chất từng cái. Mã màu ANSI `%highlight`/`%cyan` trong `PatternLayoutEncoder` là để mắt người đọc trên terminal dev — production không có ai ngồi đọc terminal, nên cho sink file/production một encoder JSON riêng (ví dụ `LogstashEncoder`, đúng như cặp console/file bài học này dạy) thay vì dùng lại encoder pattern có màu; việc tô màu để dành cho phía đọc log (log viewer, Loki/Grafana) chứ không tốn CPU serialize escape-code ANSI ở hot path. `SyncFileAppender.append()` gọi `writer.write(...)` rồi `writer.flush()` trên chính thread gọi là I/O đồng bộ — mỗi request được log là một lần chặn đúng Tomcat worker thread đang xử lý request đó, chờ đĩa, dồn dập ở endpoint log nhiều thì y hệt một node bị cạn thread pool; bọc nó trong `AsyncAppender` sẵn có của Logback (một worker thread riêng + hàng đợi bounded) để request thread bàn giao event rồi chạy tiếp, hoặc bỏ hẳn việc tự ghi file mà log ra stdout để một collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) hút đi — request thread không còn chờ đĩa. Cuối cùng, `ContentCachingRequestWrapper`/`ContentCachingResponseWrapper` buffer rồi log cả body request/response ở info trên MỌI request là chi phí serialize + payload lớn nhất trong ba cái: hầu hết request thành công không cần body đầy đủ để debug, nên hạ xuống level policy (chỉ log full body khi có lỗi), sampling (1/N request bình thường), hoặc field allow-list (chỉ vài field nghiệp vụ cần). Sau khi đổi, phải load test lại đo p99/CPU để xác nhận có cải thiện thật, và theo dõi log volume không tụt quá đà. Đánh đổi phải nói thẳng: mỗi lần cắt là mất một phần khả năng debug — cách hợp lý là giữ full detail ở nhánh lỗi (nơi bạn thực sự cần điều tra) và chỉ cắt gọn ở nhánh happy path, chứ không cắt đều tay khắp nơi.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure before changing anything: isolate each cost by turning off the colored pattern encoder, `SyncFileAppender`, and body logging one at a time, then re-measure p99/CPU — because the three have a different nature (CPU-bound formatting, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization), so \"fixing all three at once\" leaves you not knowing which one actually mattered. Once you know which dominates, fix each according to its own nature. The `%highlight`/`%cyan` ANSI codes in `PatternLayoutEncoder` exist for a human's eyes on the dev terminal — nobody is watching a terminal in production, so give the file/production sink its own JSON encoder (e.g. `LogstashEncoder`, exactly the console/file split this module teaches) instead of reusing the colored pattern encoder; the pretty-printing work belongs on the reader side (a log viewer, Loki/Grafana), not burning CPU serializing ANSI escape codes on the hot path. `SyncFileAppender.append()` calling `writer.write(...)` then `writer.flush()` on the calling thread is synchronous I/O — every logged request blocks the exact Tomcat worker thread handling it, waiting on disk, and on a log-heavy endpoint that piles up exactly like a thread-pool-starved node; wrap it in Logback's own `AsyncAppender` (a dedicated worker thread plus a bounded queue) so the request thread hands off the event and moves on, or better, drop the homegrown file write entirely and log to stdout for a collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) to pick up — the request thread no longer waits on disk. Finally, `ContentCachingRequestWrapper`/`ContentCachingResponseWrapper` buffering and logging both the request and response body at info on EVERY request is the largest serialization + payload cost of the three: most successful requests don't need the full body to debug, so drop it to a level policy (full body only on error), sampling (1/N normal requests), or a field allow-list (only the handful of business fields that matter). After changing anything, load-test again to confirm a real p99/CPU improvement, and watch that log volume hasn't dropped so far it hurts debugging. Be honest about the trade-off: every cut costs some real debugging power — the sane split is full detail on the error path (where you actually need to investigate) and a trimmed happy path, not an even cut everywhere."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐầu tiên đo trước khi đổi bất cứ gì: cô lập từng cost bằng cách tắt lần lượt theme tô màu, `SyncFileSink`, và body logging rồi đo lại p99/CPU — vì ba cái này có bản chất khác nhau (CPU-bound formatting, thread-blocking I/O, allocation-bound buffering) nên \"sửa cả ba cùng lúc\" khiến bạn không biết cái nào thực sự đáng công. Sau khi biết cái nào chiếm ưu thế, sửa theo đúng bản chất từng cái. `AnsiConsoleTheme.Code` là để mắt người đọc trên terminal dev — không ai đọc mã ANSI màu từ một file log, nên bỏ theme này ở file sink và dùng `CompactJsonFormatter` của Serilog thay vào (đúng như bài học đã wire) — việc tô màu/format đẹp để dành cho phía đọc log (Seq, Loki/Grafana) chứ không tốn công trên hot path. `SyncFileSink.Emit` gọi `File.AppendAllText` bên trong `lock` là I/O đồng bộ chạy ngay trên thread gọi nó — ASP.NET Core xử lý request bằng thread-pool, nên mỗi request được log là một lần chiếm một worker thread chờ đĩa, dồn dập ở endpoint log nhiều thì y hệt một thread-pool bị đói; đổi sang `WriteTo.File` built-in có buffer sẵn của Serilog (hoặc bọc thêm `Serilog.Sinks.Async`), hoặc tốt hơn là bỏ hẳn sink tự viết mà log ra stdout để một collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) hút đi — thread xử lý request không còn phải chờ đĩa nữa. Cuối cùng, `RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware` buffer và log cả hai body ở `LogInformation` trên MỌI request là chi phí allocation + serialize lớn nhất trong ba cái: hầu hết request thành công không cần body đầy đủ để debug, nên hạ xuống level policy (chỉ log full body khi response là lỗi), sampling (1/N request bình thường), hoặc field allow-list (chỉ vài field nghiệp vụ cần) — và trên happy path khi không log body nữa thì cũng nên bỏ luôn `EnableBuffering()`, vì bản thân việc buffer cũng tốn memory. Sau khi đổi, phải load test lại đo p99/CPU để xác nhận có cải thiện thật, và theo dõi log volume không tụt quá đà. Đánh đổi phải nói thẳng: mỗi lần cắt là mất một phần khả năng debug — cách hợp lý là giữ full detail ở nhánh lỗi (nơi bạn thực sự cần điều tra) và chỉ cắt gọn ở nhánh happy path, chứ không cắt đều tay khắp nơi.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure before changing anything: isolate each cost by turning off the colored theme, the `SyncFileSink`, and body logging one at a time, then re-measure p99/CPU — because the three have a different nature (CPU-bound formatting, thread-blocking I/O, allocation-bound buffering), so \"fixing all three at once\" leaves you not knowing which one actually mattered. Once you know which dominates, fix each according to its own nature. `AnsiConsoleTheme.Code` exists for a human's eyes on the dev terminal — nobody reads colored ANSI codes off a log file, so drop the theme on the file sink and emit Serilog's `CompactJsonFormatter` there instead (exactly as the lesson wires it); the pretty-printing work belongs on the reader side (Seq, Loki/Grafana), not on the hot path. `SyncFileSink.Emit` calling `File.AppendAllText` inside a `lock` is synchronous I/O running on the calling thread — ASP.NET Core services requests off the thread-pool, so every logged request ties up a worker thread waiting on disk, and on a log-heavy endpoint that starves the pool exactly like a saturated connection pool; switch to Serilog's built-in buffered `WriteTo.File` (or wrap it with `Serilog.Sinks.Async`), or better, drop the homegrown sink entirely and log to stdout for a collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) to pick up — the request thread no longer waits on disk. Finally, `RequestBodyLoggingMiddleware` buffering and logging both bodies at `LogInformation` on EVERY request is the largest allocation + serialization cost of the three: most successful requests don't need the full body to debug, so drop it to a level policy (full body only when the response is an error status), sampling (1/N normal requests), or a field allow-list (only the handful of business fields that matter) — and on the happy path, once the body isn't logged, drop the `EnableBuffering()` call too, since the buffering itself has a memory cost. After changing anything, load-test again to confirm a real p99/CPU improvement, and watch that log volume hasn't dropped so far it hurts debugging. Be honest about the trade-off: every cut costs some real debugging power — the sane split is full detail on the error path (where you actually need to investigate) and a trimmed happy path, not an even cut everywhere."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐầu tiên đo trước khi đổi bất cứ gì: cô lập từng cost bằng cách lần lượt đổi sang JSON encoder thuần, rồi sang file writer có buffer/async, rồi bỏ body logging — từng cái một — và đo lại p99/CPU sau mỗi lần, vì ba cái này có bản chất khác nhau (CPU-bound encoding, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization) nên \"sửa cả ba cùng lúc\" khiến bạn không biết cái nào thực sự đáng công. Sau khi biết cái nào chiếm ưu thế, sửa theo đúng bản chất từng cái. `zapcore.CapitalColorLevelEncoder` bên trong `consoleEncoder` là để mắt người đọc trên terminal dev — nó bọc mỗi level trong ANSI escape code, và tệ hơn, đoạn code này dùng LẠI đúng encoder đó cho file core, nên aggregator cuối cùng phải cố parse escape-code rác thay vì JSON sạch; cho file core một `zapcore.NewJSONEncoder` riêng, và chỉ bật `consoleEncoder` trên stdout khi có check env (hoặc bỏ hẳn console core ở production, vì không ai ngồi đọc terminal ở đó). `syncFileWriter.Write` mở, append, rồi đóng file ở mỗi lần gọi — đó là một syscall chặn gồm open + write + close ngay trên request path cho mỗi dòng log, và ở endpoint log nhiều thì dồn dập y hệt một goroutine bị nghẽn I/O; thay bằng một `*os.File` mở một lần lúc khởi động rồi bọc trong một `zapcore.WriteSyncer` có buffer (hoặc dùng sink kiểu lumberjack tích hợp sẵn), hoặc tốt hơn, bỏ hẳn việc tự ghi file mà log ra stdout để một collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) tail lấy — goroutine xử lý request không còn chờ đĩa. Cuối cùng, log cả body request/response qua `zap.ByteString` ở info trên MỌI request là chi phí allocation + serialize lớn nhất trong ba cái: hầu hết request thành công không cần body đầy đủ để debug, nên hạ xuống level policy (chỉ log full body khi lỗi, đặt `zap.ByteString` trong nhánh `if status >= 500`), sampling (1/N request bình thường), hoặc field allow-list (chỉ vài field nghiệp vụ cần). Sau khi đổi, phải load test lại đo p99/CPU để xác nhận có cải thiện thật, và theo dõi log volume không tụt quá đà. Đánh đổi phải nói thẳng: mỗi lần cắt là mất một phần khả năng debug — cách hợp lý là giữ full detail ở nhánh lỗi (nơi bạn thực sự cần điều tra) và chỉ cắt gọn ở nhánh happy path, chứ không cắt đều tay khắp nơi.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure before changing anything: isolate each cost by swapping to a plain JSON encoder, then to a buffered/async file writer, then dropping body logging — one at a time — and re-measure p99/CPU after each, because the three have a different nature (CPU-bound encoding, I/O-bound blocking, allocation-bound serialization), so \"fixing all three at once\" leaves you not knowing which one actually mattered. Once you know which dominates, fix each according to its own nature. `zapcore.CapitalColorLevelEncoder` inside `consoleEncoder` exists for a human's eyes on the dev terminal — it wraps every level in ANSI escape codes, and worse, this code reuses that SAME encoder on the file core, so the aggregator ends up trying to parse escape-code garbage instead of clean JSON; give the file core its own `zapcore.NewJSONEncoder`, and gate `consoleEncoder` on stdout behind an env check (or drop the console core entirely in production, since nobody's watching a terminal there). `syncFileWriter.Write` opens, appends, and closes the file on every single call — that's a blocking syscall doing open + write + close on the request path for every log line, and on a log-heavy endpoint that piles up exactly like an I/O-starved goroutine; replace it with a `*os.File` opened once at startup wrapped in a buffered `zapcore.WriteSyncer` (or `zap.NewJSON`'s built-in lumberjack-style sink), or better, drop the homegrown file write entirely and log to stdout for a collector/sidecar (Fluentd, Vector, Promtail) to tail — the request goroutine no longer waits on disk. Finally, logging both the request and response body via `zap.ByteString` at info on EVERY request is the largest allocation + serialization cost of the three: most successful requests don't need the full body to debug, so drop it to a level policy (full body only on error, via `zap.ByteString` inside an `if status >= 500` branch), sampling (1/N normal requests), or a field allow-list (only the handful of business fields that matter). After changing anything, load-test again to confirm a real p99/CPU improvement, and watch that log volume hasn't dropped so far it hurts debugging. Be honest about the trade-off: every cut costs some real debugging power — the sane split is full detail on the error path (where you actually need to investigate) and a trimmed happy path, not an even cut everywhere."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation', '231d8cc57-stem1', '0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation/12', 12, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'optimize', ARRAY['injection-scope', 'scope-propagation', 'execution-scoped-context']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript/NestJS - khái niệm áp dụng cho mọi stack.) Đoạn code dưới đây chạy đúng, nhưng chậm: throughput tụt hẳn sau khi một dev đặt TenantContext ở request-scope để lưu ngữ cảnh tenant riêng cho mỗi request, dù business logic không đổi một dòng. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích cơ chế đằng sau sự chậm này: vì sao request-scope lại 'lan' lên cả chuỗi phụ thuộc, chi phí thực sự phát sinh ở đâu, và bạn sẽ viết lại thế nào để kéo throughput về trong khi vẫn mang được ngữ cảnh tenant theo từng request?","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })\nexport class TenantContext {\n  tenantId: string;\n}\n\n@Injectable() // no explicit scope, but inherits REQUEST because it depends on TenantContext\nexport class LoggerService {\n  constructor(private readonly tenantContext: TenantContext) {}\n  log(message: string) {\n    console.log(`[tenant:${this.tenantContext.tenantId}] ${message}`);\n  }\n}\n\n@Injectable() // inherits REQUEST too — depends on LoggerService, which depends on TenantContext\nexport class OrderService {\n  constructor(private readonly logger: LoggerService) {}\n  async createOrder(dto: CreateOrderDto) {\n    this.logger.log('creating order');\n    return this.orderRepo.save(dto); // business logic unchanged\n  }\n}\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated via TypeScript/NestJS - the concept applies to any stack.) The code below runs correctly, but it is slow: throughput dropped sharply after a dev put TenantContext at request-scope to hold per-request tenant context, even though the business logic didn't change by a single line. Read the given code, then explain the mechanism behind the slowdown: why request-scope 'propagates' up the whole dependency chain, where the real cost arises, and how you'd rewrite it to bring throughput back while still carrying per-request tenant context.","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })\nexport class TenantContext {\n  tenantId: string;\n}\n\n@Injectable() // no explicit scope, but inherits REQUEST because it depends on TenantContext\nexport class LoggerService {\n  constructor(private readonly tenantContext: TenantContext) {}\n  log(message: string) {\n    console.log(`[tenant:${this.tenantContext.tenantId}] ${message}`);\n  }\n}\n\n@Injectable() // inherits REQUEST too — depends on LoggerService, which depends on TenantContext\nexport class OrderService {\n  constructor(private readonly logger: LoggerService) {}\n  async createOrder(dto: CreateOrderDto) {\n    this.logger.log('creating order');\n    return this.orderRepo.save(dto); // business logic unchanged\n  }\n}\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"Đoạn code dưới đây chạy đúng, nhưng chậm: throughput tụt hẳn sau khi một dev đặt TenantContext ở request scope để lưu ngữ cảnh tenant riêng cho mỗi request. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích cơ chế đằng sau sự chậm này: vì sao scope cả chuỗi thành request lại buộc phải khởi tạo lại mỗi request thay vì dùng scoped-proxy mà Spring đã có sẵn cho đúng việc này, chi phí thực sự phát sinh ở đâu, và bạn sẽ viết lại thế nào để kéo throughput về trong khi vẫn mang được ngữ cảnh tenant theo từng request?","givenCode":"```java\n@Component\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)\npublic class TenantContext {\n    private String tenantId;\n    public String getTenantId() { return tenantId; }\n    public void setTenantId(String tenantId) { this.tenantId = tenantId; }\n}\n\n@Service\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS) // thêm vào để luôn thấy TenantContext \"sống\", không phải proxy cũ\npublic class LoggerService {\n    private final TenantContext tenantContext;\n\n    public LoggerService(TenantContext tenantContext) {\n        this.tenantContext = tenantContext;\n    }\n\n    public void log(String message) {\n        System.out.printf(\"[tenant:%s] %s%n\", tenantContext.getTenantId(), message);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS) // cùng lý do — đứng ngay sau LoggerService trong chuỗi\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final LoggerService logger;\n    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;\n\n    public OrderService(LoggerService logger, OrderRepository orderRepo) {\n        this.logger = logger;\n        this.orderRepo = orderRepo;\n    }\n\n    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderDto dto) {\n        logger.log(\"creating order\");\n        return orderRepo.save(dto.toEntity()); // business logic không đổi\n    }\n}\n// Sau thay đổi này: p99 tăng gấp đôi, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. Read the given code, then explain the mechanism behind the slowdown: why scoping the whole chain to request forces per-request re-instantiation instead of using the scoped-proxy Spring already provides for exactly this, where the real cost arises, and how you'd rewrite it to bring throughput back while still carrying per-request tenant context.","givenCode":"```java\n@Component\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)\npublic class TenantContext {\n    private String tenantId;\n    public String getTenantId() { return tenantId; }\n    public void setTenantId(String tenantId) { this.tenantId = tenantId; }\n}\n\n@Service\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS) // added so it always sees the live TenantContext, not a stale proxy target\npublic class LoggerService {\n    private final TenantContext tenantContext;\n\n    public LoggerService(TenantContext tenantContext) {\n        this.tenantContext = tenantContext;\n    }\n\n    public void log(String message) {\n        System.out.printf(\"[tenant:%s] %s%n\", tenantContext.getTenantId(), message);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\n@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS) // same reasoning — it sits right after LoggerService in the chain\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final LoggerService logger;\n    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;\n\n    public OrderService(LoggerService logger, OrderRepository orderRepo) {\n        this.logger = logger;\n        this.orderRepo = orderRepo;\n    }\n\n    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderDto dto) {\n        logger.log(\"creating order\");\n        return orderRepo.save(dto.toEntity()); // business logic unchanged\n    }\n}\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"Đoạn code dưới đây chạy đúng, nhưng chậm: throughput tụt hẳn sau khi một dev đăng ký TenantContext bằng AddScoped để lưu ngữ cảnh tenant riêng cho mỗi request, dù business logic không đổi một dòng. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích cơ chế đằng sau sự chậm này: vì sao việc LoggerService/OrderService phụ thuộc vào TenantContext lại buộc chúng phải mang cùng lifetime scoped đó, chi phí thực sự phát sinh ở đâu, và bạn sẽ viết lại thế nào để kéo throughput về trong khi vẫn mang được ngữ cảnh tenant theo từng request?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// TenantContext.cs — đăng ký AddScoped, một instance cho mỗi HTTP request\npublic class TenantContext\n{\n    public string? TenantId { get; set; }\n}\n\n// TenantMiddleware.cs\npublic class TenantMiddleware\n{\n    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;\n\n    public TenantMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;\n\n    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext httpContext, TenantContext tenantContext)\n    {\n        tenantContext.TenantId = httpContext.Request.Headers[\"X-Tenant-Id\"].FirstOrDefault() ?? \"default\";\n        await _next(httpContext);\n    }\n}\n\n// LoggerService.cs — buộc phải AddScoped: đăng ký AddSingleton ở đây sẽ throw ngay lúc khởi động\npublic class LoggerService\n{\n    private readonly TenantContext _tenantContext;\n\n    public LoggerService(TenantContext tenantContext) => _tenantContext = tenantContext;\n\n    public void Log(string message) =>\n        Console.WriteLine($\"[tenant:{_tenantContext.TenantId}] {message}\");\n}\n\n// OrderService.cs — cũng phải AddScoped, vì phụ thuộc LoggerService, mà LoggerService lại phụ thuộc TenantContext\npublic class OrderService\n{\n    private readonly LoggerService _logger;\n    private readonly AppDbContext _db;\n\n    public OrderService(LoggerService logger, AppDbContext db)\n    {\n        _logger = logger;\n        _db = db;\n    }\n\n    public async Task CreateOrderAsync(CreateOrderDto dto)\n    {\n        _logger.Log(\"creating order\");\n        await _db.Orders.AddAsync(dto.ToEntity());\n        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); // business logic unchanged\n    }\n}\n\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<TenantContext>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<LoggerService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<OrderService>();\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"The code below runs correctly, but it is slow: throughput dropped sharply after a dev registered TenantContext with AddScoped to hold per-request tenant context, even though the business logic didn't change by a single line. Read the given code, then explain the mechanism behind the slowdown: why does LoggerService/OrderService depending on TenantContext force them into that same scoped lifetime, where does the real cost arise, and how would you rewrite it to bring throughput back while still carrying per-request tenant context?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// TenantContext.cs — registered AddScoped, one instance per HTTP request\npublic class TenantContext\n{\n    public string? TenantId { get; set; }\n}\n\n// TenantMiddleware.cs\npublic class TenantMiddleware\n{\n    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;\n\n    public TenantMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;\n\n    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext httpContext, TenantContext tenantContext)\n    {\n        tenantContext.TenantId = httpContext.Request.Headers[\"X-Tenant-Id\"].FirstOrDefault() ?? \"default\";\n        await _next(httpContext);\n    }\n}\n\n// LoggerService.cs — forced to AddScoped: registering AddSingleton here throws at startup\npublic class LoggerService\n{\n    private readonly TenantContext _tenantContext;\n\n    public LoggerService(TenantContext tenantContext) => _tenantContext = tenantContext;\n\n    public void Log(string message) =>\n        Console.WriteLine($\"[tenant:{_tenantContext.TenantId}] {message}\");\n}\n\n// OrderService.cs — AddScoped too, because it depends on LoggerService, which depends on TenantContext\npublic class OrderService\n{\n    private readonly LoggerService _logger;\n    private readonly AppDbContext _db;\n\n    public OrderService(LoggerService logger, AppDbContext db)\n    {\n        _logger = logger;\n        _db = db;\n    }\n\n    public async Task CreateOrderAsync(CreateOrderDto dto)\n    {\n        _logger.Log(\"creating order\");\n        await _db.Orders.AddAsync(dto.ToEntity());\n        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); // business logic unchanged\n    }\n}\n\n// Program.cs\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<TenantContext>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<LoggerService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<OrderService>();\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh họa bằng Go. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích cơ chế đằng sau sự chậm này: vì sao việc gắn tenant context làm field của struct lại buộc cả chuỗi phụ thuộc phải khởi tạo lại theo từng request, chi phí thực sự phát sinh ở đâu, và bạn sẽ viết lại thế nào để kéo throughput về trong khi vẫn mang được ngữ cảnh tenant theo từng request?","givenCode":"```go\ntype TenantContext struct {\n\tTenantID string\n}\n\ntype LoggerService struct {\n\ttenant *TenantContext // per-request state baked directly into the service\n}\n\nfunc NewLoggerService(t *TenantContext) *LoggerService {\n\treturn &LoggerService{tenant: t}\n}\n\nfunc (s *LoggerService) Log(message string) {\n\tfmt.Printf(\"[tenant:%s] %s\\n\", s.tenant.TenantID, message)\n}\n\ntype OrderService struct {\n\tlogger *LoggerService\n}\n\nfunc NewOrderService(l *LoggerService) *OrderService {\n\treturn &OrderService{logger: l}\n}\n\nfunc (s *OrderService) CreateOrder(dto CreateOrderDto) error {\n\ts.logger.Log(\"creating order\")\n\treturn orderRepo.Save(dto) // business logic unchanged\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n\tr := gin.Default()\n\tr.POST(\"/orders\", func(c *gin.Context) {\n\t\t// rebuilds the whole chain on every request just to carry tenantId\n\t\ttenantCtx := &TenantContext{TenantID: c.GetHeader(\"X-Tenant-Id\")}\n\t\tloggerSvc := NewLoggerService(tenantCtx) // fresh instance every request\n\t\torderSvc := NewOrderService(loggerSvc)   // fresh instance every request\n\n\t\tvar dto CreateOrderDto\n\t\tc.BindJSON(&dto)\n\t\torderSvc.CreateOrder(dto)\n\t\tc.JSON(200, gin.H{\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\t})\n\tr.Run(\":3000\")\n}\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go. Read the given code, then explain the mechanism behind the slowdown: why baking tenant context into a struct field forces the whole dependency chain to be reconstructed per request, where the real cost arises, and how you'd rewrite it to bring throughput back while still carrying per-request tenant context.","givenCode":"```go\ntype TenantContext struct {\n\tTenantID string\n}\n\ntype LoggerService struct {\n\ttenant *TenantContext // per-request state baked directly into the service\n}\n\nfunc NewLoggerService(t *TenantContext) *LoggerService {\n\treturn &LoggerService{tenant: t}\n}\n\nfunc (s *LoggerService) Log(message string) {\n\tfmt.Printf(\"[tenant:%s] %s\\n\", s.tenant.TenantID, message)\n}\n\ntype OrderService struct {\n\tlogger *LoggerService\n}\n\nfunc NewOrderService(l *LoggerService) *OrderService {\n\treturn &OrderService{logger: l}\n}\n\nfunc (s *OrderService) CreateOrder(dto CreateOrderDto) error {\n\ts.logger.Log(\"creating order\")\n\treturn orderRepo.Save(dto) // business logic unchanged\n}\n\nfunc main() {\n\tr := gin.Default()\n\tr.POST(\"/orders\", func(c *gin.Context) {\n\t\t// rebuilds the whole chain on every request just to carry tenantId\n\t\ttenantCtx := &TenantContext{TenantID: c.GetHeader(\"X-Tenant-Id\")}\n\t\tloggerSvc := NewLoggerService(tenantCtx) // fresh instance every request\n\t\torderSvc := NewOrderService(loggerSvc)   // fresh instance every request\n\n\t\tvar dto CreateOrderDto\n\t\tc.BindJSON(&dto)\n\t\torderSvc.CreateOrder(dto)\n\t\tc.JSON(200, gin.H{\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\t})\n\tr.Run(\":3000\")\n}\n// After this change: p99 doubled, throughput -40%\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Cơ chế lưu ngữ cảnh cô lập-theo-đơn-vị-thực-thi bạn vừa đề xuất (AsyncLocalStorage / ThreadLocal / AsyncLocal<T> / `context.Context`) lưu context theo từng lần thực thi — có bẫy nào khiến context bị mất hoặc lẫn giữa các request khi dùng nó không?","Nếu buộc phải dùng per-request DI scope thật, bạn giới hạn \"vùng lan\" của nó thế nào để không kéo cả pipeline theo?"],"hints":["Hỏi: nếu A phụ thuộc B, mà B phải \"tươi\" mỗi request, thì container còn chia sẻ được một A singleton cho mọi request nữa không?","Chi phí không đến từ business logic (nó không đổi) mà từ *việc tạo lại instance*. Có cách nào mang ngữ cảnh theo request mà không phải tạo lại provider mỗi lần không?"]},"en":{"followUps":["The execution-scoped context mechanism you just proposed (AsyncLocalStorage / ThreadLocal / AsyncLocal<T> / `context.Context`) stores context per unit of execution — are there pitfalls where that context gets lost or bleeds across requests?","If you were forced to use real per-request DI scope, how would you bound its \"propagation area\" so it doesn't drag the whole pipeline along?"],"hints":["Ask: if A depends on B, and B must be \"fresh\" per request, can the container still share one singleton A across all requests?","The cost isn't from the business logic (it's unchanged) but from *recreating instances*. Is there a way to carry context per request without recreating a provider each time?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[technical] Nêu đúng mặc định của DI container: một instance dùng chung toàn app (singleton), rẻ và đủ cho service stateless — đây là baseline bị phá vỡ khi một provider được khai báo per-request-scoped để giữ ngữ cảnh riêng theo từng request.","[technical] Giải thích đúng cơ chế \"lan\" scope: một provider per-request-scoped buộc MỌI thứ phụ thuộc nó (trực tiếp hay gián tiếp) cũng phải trở thành per-request theo — container không còn phát được một singleton dùng chung cho cả nhánh phụ thuộc đó nữa.","[problemSolving] Chỉ đúng chi phí thực: mỗi request giờ phải khởi tạo lại toàn bộ chuỗi các đối tượng phụ thuộc thay vì tái dùng các instance singleton đã dựng sẵn — chi phí tạo lại/resolve lặp lại này chính là thứ kéo tăng latency và kéo giảm throughput, dù business logic không đổi.","[technical] Đề xuất cách sửa đúng: mang ngữ cảnh theo-từng-request qua một cơ chế lưu trữ cô lập-theo-đơn-vị-thực-thi (execution-scoped storage — ví dụ AsyncLocalStorage ở Node, ThreadLocal ở Java, AsyncLocal<T> ở C#, hoặc truyền tường minh `context.Context` ở Go) thay vì khai per-request scope trên provider — để mọi thứ phụ thuộc trong chuỗi trở lại singleton.","[communication] Trình bày rõ khi nào per-request DI scope thật sự chính đáng (state tuyệt đối không được rò rỉ hoặc lẫn giữa các request, không còn cách nào khác để cô lập) so với khi nó chỉ là tiện lợi nhất thời mà âm thầm lan chi phí ra khắp chuỗi phụ thuộc, như trong tình huống này.","[testing] Xác minh cách sửa bằng load test trước/sau: sau khi provider trở lại singleton + context cô lập-theo-đơn-vị-thực-thi, chạy lại cùng một load profile và xác nhận latency lẫn throughput thật sự quay về baseline — không chỉ dừng ở \"code đọc thấy hợp lý\"."],"checklist":[{"text":"NestJS providers default to singleton scope: one instance is built at bootstrap and shared across every request, which is cheap and sufficient as long as the provider is stateless.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"Declaring a provider `Scope.REQUEST` propagates upward through the dependency graph — any provider that depends on it, directly or transitively, is forced into request scope too and loses its singleton lifetime, even if its own code never changed.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"In this code, marking `TenantContext` as request-scoped to hold per-request `tenantId` drags `LoggerService` and then `OrderService` into request scope as well, purely because they sit on the chain leading back to it.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"The real cost of the regression is not the business logic (`createOrder`'s save call is untouched) but the fact that the entire affected chain of instances now has to be re-constructed and re-resolved on every single request instead of being reused from a shared singleton.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"This repeated construction and resolution work is exactly what produces the observed doubling of p99 latency and the 40% throughput drop, since more CPU and allocation work happens per request with no change in what that request actually does.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"If a request-scoped provider sits inside the request pipeline itself, such as a guard or interceptor, the same propagation spreads even wider and the performance cost grows correspondingly larger.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The fix is to carry per-request tenant context through an execution-scoped storage mechanism instead of provider scope: AsyncLocalStorage in Node, ThreadLocal in Java, AsyncLocal<T> in C#, or explicit `context.Context` threading in Go all let contextual data travel across async boundaries without making the provider (or anything depending on it) request-scoped, so the whole chain reverts to singleton.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"Request scope is genuinely justified only when per-request state absolutely must never leak or mix across requests and no other isolation mechanism exists; using it merely as a convenient way to carry contextual data is what silently propagates reconstruction cost through the dependency graph, as it does here.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"Fixing the scope alone is not sufficient proof of resolution — the fix should be validated by re-running the same load profile used to surface the regression and confirming that p99 latency and throughput actually return to baseline, not just that the diff looks cleaner on review.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":9}],"keywords":["dependency injection scope","per-request scope propagation","singleton reuse","execution-scoped context","throughput regression"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"By default, NestJS providers are singletons — built once at bootstrap and shared across every request, which is fine as long as they're stateless. The problem is that marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates upward through the whole dependency graph, so anything that depends on it, directly or transitively, gets forced into request scope too, even if its own code never changed. Here, making TenantContext request-scoped to hold per-request tenantId drags LoggerService and then OrderService into request scope purely because they sit on that chain. The real cost isn't the business logic — createOrder's save call is untouched — it's that the entire chain now has to be reconstructed and re-resolved on every request instead of reused from a shared singleton, and that's exactly what's producing the doubled p99 and the 40% throughput drop. If a request-scoped provider sat inside the pipeline itself, like a guard or interceptor, this would spread even wider. The fix is to carry tenantId through AsyncLocalStorage instead of provider scope, so the whole chain reverts to singleton. Request scope is only justified when isolation absolutely can't leak and there's no other mechanism — using it just for convenience is what caused this. I'd verify by re-running the same load profile and confirming p99 and throughput return to baseline.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates upward through the dependency graph, so anything depending on it — directly or transitively — gets pulled into request scope too, even without any change to its own code. In this case, TenantContext being request-scoped to hold tenantId drags LoggerService and then OrderService along with it, just because they're on that chain. The actual cost isn't the business logic itself, since createOrder's save call is untouched — it's that the whole chain now has to be rebuilt and re-resolved on every single request instead of reused as a singleton. The fix is to carry the per-request tenantId through AsyncLocalStorage instead of provider scope, so the whole chain goes back to singleton. Request scope should really only be used when isolation genuinely can't leak and there's no other option — using it just for convenience is what caused this regression. I'd confirm the fix by re-running the same load test and checking p99 and throughput are back to baseline.","sonnet":73,"haiku":73},{"level":3,"answer":"The issue is that marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates through the dependency graph, so anything depending on it gets pulled into request scope too. Here TenantContext being request-scoped drags LoggerService and then OrderService along with it since they're on that chain. The real cost is that the whole chain has to be rebuilt on every request rather than reused as a singleton — the actual business logic in createOrder wasn't touched at all.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the main cost here is just that some of these objects are getting rebuilt more often than they need to be on each request, instead of being reused, which probably adds some extra overhead.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably look at adding more caching around the database queries, or maybe increasing the connection pool size, since that's usually what causes slowdowns like this under load.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[technical] States the DI container's default correctly: one instance shared app-wide (singleton), cheap and enough for stateless services — this is the baseline broken once a provider is declared per-request-scoped to hold context specific to each request.","[technical] Explains the scope-propagation mechanism correctly: a per-request-scoped provider forces EVERYTHING that depends on it (directly or transitively) to become per-request too — the container can no longer hand out a shared singleton for that whole dependency branch.","[problemSolving] Pinpoints the real cost: every request must now re-construct the entire chain of dependent objects instead of reusing pre-built singleton instances — this repeated construction/resolution cost is exactly what drives latency up and throughput down, even though the business logic is unchanged.","[technical] Proposes the correct fix: carry per-request context through an execution-scoped storage mechanism (e.g. AsyncLocalStorage on Node, ThreadLocal on Java, AsyncLocal<T> on C#, or explicitly threading `context.Context` on Go) instead of declaring per-request scope on the provider — so everything depending on it in the chain reverts to singleton.","[communication] Clearly frames when per-request DI scope is genuinely justified (state that must absolutely never leak or mix across requests, with no other way to isolate it) versus when it's just a momentary convenience that silently propagates cost across the whole dependency chain, as it did here.","[testing] Verifies the fix with a before/after load test: once the provider is back to singleton plus an execution-scoped context mechanism, re-run the same load profile and confirm both latency and throughput actually return to baseline — not just that \"the code looks right\"."],"checklist":[{"text":"NestJS providers default to singleton scope: one instance is built at bootstrap and shared across every request, which is cheap and sufficient as long as the provider is stateless.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"Declaring a provider `Scope.REQUEST` propagates upward through the dependency graph — any provider that depends on it, directly or transitively, is forced into request scope too and loses its singleton lifetime, even if its own code never changed.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"In this code, marking `TenantContext` as request-scoped to hold per-request `tenantId` drags `LoggerService` and then `OrderService` into request scope as well, purely because they sit on the chain leading back to it.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"The real cost of the regression is not the business logic (`createOrder`'s save call is untouched) but the fact that the entire affected chain of instances now has to be re-constructed and re-resolved on every single request instead of being reused from a shared singleton.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"This repeated construction and resolution work is exactly what produces the observed doubling of p99 latency and the 40% throughput drop, since more CPU and allocation work happens per request with no change in what that request actually does.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"If a request-scoped provider sits inside the request pipeline itself, such as a guard or interceptor, the same propagation spreads even wider and the performance cost grows correspondingly larger.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The fix is to carry per-request tenant context through an execution-scoped storage mechanism instead of provider scope: AsyncLocalStorage in Node, ThreadLocal in Java, AsyncLocal<T> in C#, or explicit `context.Context` threading in Go all let contextual data travel across async boundaries without making the provider (or anything depending on it) request-scoped, so the whole chain reverts to singleton.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"Request scope is genuinely justified only when per-request state absolutely must never leak or mix across requests and no other isolation mechanism exists; using it merely as a convenient way to carry contextual data is what silently propagates reconstruction cost through the dependency graph, as it does here.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"Fixing the scope alone is not sufficient proof of resolution — the fix should be validated by re-running the same load profile used to surface the regression and confirming that p99 latency and throughput actually return to baseline, not just that the diff looks cleaner on review.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":9}],"keywords":["dependency injection scope","per-request scope propagation","singleton reuse","execution-scoped context","throughput regression"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"By default, NestJS providers are singletons — built once at bootstrap and shared across every request, which is fine as long as they're stateless. The problem is that marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates upward through the whole dependency graph, so anything that depends on it, directly or transitively, gets forced into request scope too, even if its own code never changed. Here, making TenantContext request-scoped to hold per-request tenantId drags LoggerService and then OrderService into request scope purely because they sit on that chain. The real cost isn't the business logic — createOrder's save call is untouched — it's that the entire chain now has to be reconstructed and re-resolved on every request instead of reused from a shared singleton, and that's exactly what's producing the doubled p99 and the 40% throughput drop. If a request-scoped provider sat inside the pipeline itself, like a guard or interceptor, this would spread even wider. The fix is to carry tenantId through AsyncLocalStorage instead of provider scope, so the whole chain reverts to singleton. Request scope is only justified when isolation absolutely can't leak and there's no other mechanism — using it just for convenience is what caused this. I'd verify by re-running the same load profile and confirming p99 and throughput return to baseline.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates upward through the dependency graph, so anything depending on it — directly or transitively — gets pulled into request scope too, even without any change to its own code. In this case, TenantContext being request-scoped to hold tenantId drags LoggerService and then OrderService along with it, just because they're on that chain. The actual cost isn't the business logic itself, since createOrder's save call is untouched — it's that the whole chain now has to be rebuilt and re-resolved on every single request instead of reused as a singleton. The fix is to carry the per-request tenantId through AsyncLocalStorage instead of provider scope, so the whole chain goes back to singleton. Request scope should really only be used when isolation genuinely can't leak and there's no other option — using it just for convenience is what caused this regression. I'd confirm the fix by re-running the same load test and checking p99 and throughput are back to baseline.","sonnet":73,"haiku":73},{"level":3,"answer":"The issue is that marking a provider Scope.REQUEST propagates through the dependency graph, so anything depending on it gets pulled into request scope too. Here TenantContext being request-scoped drags LoggerService and then OrderService along with it since they're on that chain. The real cost is that the whole chain has to be rebuilt on every request rather than reused as a singleton — the actual business logic in createOrder wasn't touched at all.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the main cost here is just that some of these objects are getting rebuilt more often than they need to be on each request, instead of being reused, which probably adds some extra overhead.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably look at adding more caching around the database queries, or maybe increasing the connection pool size, since that's usually what causes slowdowns like this under load.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCode cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng chậm vì injection scope, không phải vì business logic. Đọc code: `TenantContext` được gắn `@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })` để giữ `tenantId` riêng mỗi request; `LoggerService` và `OrderService` không khai báo scope tường minh, nhưng vì phụ thuộc (trực tiếp hay gián tiếp) vào `TenantContext`, NestJS *lan* `Scope.REQUEST` lên cả chuỗi — container không còn phát được một singleton dùng chung cho nhánh đó nữa. Chi phí thực nằm ở đó: thay vì tái dùng instance `LoggerService`/`OrderService` đã dựng sẵn, mỗi request giờ phải allocate và resolve lại toàn bộ nhánh dependency graph đó — chi phí khởi tạo lặp lại này chính là thứ khiến p99 tăng gấp đôi và throughput giảm 40%, dù logic của `createOrder` không đổi. Nếu provider đó nằm trong chuỗi pipeline (guard/interceptor), phạm vi lan còn rộng hơn và càng đau. Cách cân nhắc: request-scope nên là *ngoại lệ*, chỉ dùng khi state per-request tuyệt đối không được lẫn và không còn cách nào khác. Với nhu cầu \"mang ngữ cảnh tenant theo request\", dùng AsyncLocalStorage thay thế: lưu `tenantId` theo một async execution và đọc ra qua ranh giới async mà KHÔNG cần biến `TenantContext` (hay bất cứ thứ gì phụ thuộc nó) thành request-scope — `LoggerService` và `OrderService` trở lại singleton, throughput hồi phục, mà vẫn có context per-request ở nơi cần. Cách sửa không dừng ở việc đọc code thấy hợp lý — phải xác minh bằng load test trước/sau: chạy lại cùng load profile trên bản singleton + ALS và xác nhận p99 lẫn throughput thật sự quay về baseline, chứ không chỉ code đọc sạch hơn.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given code runs correctly but is slow because of injection scope, not business logic. Reading it: `TenantContext` is decorated `@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })` to hold per-request `tenantId`; `LoggerService` and `OrderService` don't declare an explicit scope, but because they depend (directly or transitively) on `TenantContext`, NestJS *propagates* `Scope.REQUEST` up the whole chain — the container can no longer hand out a shared singleton for that branch. That's where the real cost lives: instead of reusing pre-built `LoggerService`/`OrderService` instances, every request now allocates and re-resolves that entire branch of the dependency graph — this repeated construction cost is exactly what doubles p99 and cuts throughput 40%, even though `createOrder`'s business logic is untouched. If that provider sat inside the pipeline (a guard/interceptor), the propagation would be even wider and hurt more. How to weigh it: request-scope should be the *exception*, used only when per-request state must absolutely never mix and there's no other way. For \"carry tenant context per request\", use AsyncLocalStorage instead: store `tenantId` per async execution and read it across async boundaries WITHOUT making `TenantContext` (or anything it touches) request-scoped — `LoggerService` and `OrderService` go back to singletons, throughput recovers, and per-request context is still available where needed. The fix isn't done on code-review alone — validate it with a before/after load test: run the same load profile against the singleton + ALS version and confirm p99 and throughput actually return to baseline, not just that the code reads cleaner."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCode cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng chậm vì cách khai báo scope, không phải vì business logic. Đọc code: `TenantContext` được gắn `@Scope(... SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)` — bản thân việc này đúng, `proxyMode` chính là cơ chế Spring dựng sẵn để một bean request-scope inject được vào bean singleton mà không cần lan scope lên trên: singleton chỉ giữ một CGLIB proxy nhẹ, và mỗi lần gọi method proxy mới tra ra instance thật của request hiện tại. Nhưng ở đây `LoggerService` và `OrderService` lại được gắn THÊM cùng annotation request-scope + proxyMode đó — có vẻ như dev nghĩ \"chạm vào tenant context thì phải scope theo\", trong khi đúng ra chỉ leaf bean (`TenantContext`) cần proxy, còn phần còn lại của chuỗi lẽ ra vẫn nên là singleton bình thường (bean không stateful thì mặc định singleton là đủ, đây chính là edge case nêu trong bài: lạm dụng request/prototype scope làm tăng chi phí khởi tạo và cần proxy). Chi phí thực nằm ở đó: thay vì tái dùng instance `LoggerService`/`OrderService` đã dựng sẵn, container giờ phải dựng lại object thật đứng sau proxy của CẢ BA bean cho mỗi request — chi phí khởi tạo lặp lại (cộng thêm overhead tra cứu qua proxy ở mỗi lời gọi) chính là thứ khiến p99 tăng gấp đôi và throughput giảm 40%, dù logic của `createOrder` không đổi. Cách cân nhắc: request scope nên là *ngoại lệ*, chỉ áp cho đúng bean giữ state per-request, và khi phải wire nó vào singleton thì `proxyMode` đã đủ — không cần lan scope lên các bean gọi nó. Với nhu cầu \"mang ngữ cảnh tenant theo request\" mà vẫn muốn né cả overhead proxy trên hot path, cách idiomatic hơn của Java là dùng `ThreadLocal`: một `TenantContext` static giữ `ThreadLocal<String>`, set giá trị trong một `Filter` (`OncePerRequestFilter`) ngay đầu request và bắt buộc `clear()` trong khối `finally` — thiếu bước clear là một lỗi rất Java-đặc-thù vì servlet container tái dùng thread pool, tenant của request trước có thể rò rỉ sang request sau nếu không dọn. Với ThreadLocal, `LoggerService` và `OrderService` quay lại là singleton hoàn toàn không annotation scope, throughput hồi phục, mà vẫn đọc được tenant hiện tại ở bất cứ đâu trong luồng xử lý request đó. Cách sửa không dừng ở việc đọc code thấy hợp lý — phải xác minh bằng load test trước/sau: chạy lại cùng load profile trên bản singleton + ThreadLocal và xác nhận p99 lẫn throughput thật sự quay về baseline, chứ không chỉ code đọc sạch hơn.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given code runs correctly but is slow because of how scope is declared, not because of business logic. Reading it: `TenantContext` carries `@Scope(... SCOPE_REQUEST, proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)` — that part is actually correct, `proxyMode` is exactly the mechanism Spring built so a request-scoped bean can be injected into a singleton WITHOUT the scope propagating upward: the singleton only holds a thin CGLIB proxy, and every method call on that proxy resolves the real current-request instance underneath. But here `LoggerService` and `OrderService` got the SAME request-scope + proxyMode annotation tacked on too — it looks like the dev assumed \"anything that touches tenant context must be scoped the same way\", when really only the leaf bean (`TenantContext`) needs the proxy; the rest of the chain should have stayed plain singleton (stateless beans default to singleton just fine — this is exactly the edge case the lesson calls out: overusing request/prototype scope raises creation cost and needs proxies). That's where the real cost lives: instead of reusing pre-built `LoggerService`/`OrderService` instances, the container now has to construct the real target object behind the proxy for ALL THREE beans on every request — that repeated construction cost, plus the extra proxy lookup indirection on every call, is exactly what doubles p99 and cuts throughput 40%, even though `createOrder`'s business logic is untouched. How to weigh it: request scope should be the *exception*, applied only to the bean that actually holds per-request state, and when it needs wiring into a singleton, `proxyMode` alone is enough — there's no need to propagate scope up to the beans calling it. For \"carry tenant context per request\" while also dodging the proxy indirection on a hot path, the more idiomatic Java route is `ThreadLocal`: a static `TenantContext` wrapping a `ThreadLocal<String>`, set inside a `Filter` (`OncePerRequestFilter`) at the start of the request and always `clear()`-ed in a `finally` block — skipping that clear is a distinctly Java gotcha, because the servlet container reuses pooled threads, so a leftover tenant ID can leak into the next request that lands on the same thread if it isn't wiped. With `ThreadLocal`, `LoggerService` and `OrderService` go back to plain singletons with no scope annotation at all, throughput recovers, and the current tenant is still readable anywhere in that request's processing thread. The fix isn't done on code-review alone — validate it with a before/after load test: run the same load profile against the singleton + ThreadLocal version and confirm p99 and throughput actually return to baseline, not just that the code reads cleaner."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCode cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng chậm vì DI lifetime, không phải vì business logic. Đọc code: `TenantContext` được đăng ký `builder.Services.AddScoped<TenantContext>()` để giữ `TenantId` riêng mỗi request; `LoggerService` nhận `TenantContext` qua constructor, nên nếu đăng ký `LoggerService` bằng `AddSingleton` thì container sẽ throw ngay lúc validate: `InvalidOperationException: Cannot consume scoped service 'TenantContext' from singleton 'LoggerService'` — built-in container của ASP.NET Core validate scope và không cho phép một singleton sống dài hơn giữ một dependency scoped sống ngắn hơn (đúng là edge case \"captive dependency\" trong bài DI). Để app còn chạy được, đội buộc phải nâng `LoggerService` lên `AddScoped` — rồi `OrderService`, vì phụ thuộc `LoggerService`, cũng phải theo vì cùng lý do. Đó chính là hình dạng của \"scope lan lên cả chuỗi\" ở C#: ASP.NET Core không tự động promote âm thầm như NestJS, nhưng validator ép tay dev nâng dần từng dependency một cho tới khi cả nhánh đều thành Scoped. Chi phí thực nằm ở đâu: ASP.NET Core tạo một DI scope mới cho mỗi HTTP request đến, và với một registration Scoped, container dựng một instance HOÀN TOÀN MỚI cho mỗi scope — nên mỗi request giờ phải khởi tạo lại từ đầu cả `TenantContext`, `LoggerService`, LẪN `OrderService` thay vì tái dùng singleton đã resolve một lần lúc khởi động. Chi phí khởi tạo lặp lại trên cả nhánh, request sau request, chính là thứ khiến p99 tăng gấp đôi và throughput giảm 40%, dù logic của `CreateOrderAsync` không hề đổi. Cách cân nhắc: Scoped nên là ngoại lệ, chỉ dành cho state thực sự per-request và không có cách nào cô lập khác (như bản thân `DbContext`) — chứ không phải chỉ để mang một chuỗi string đi xuống theo call chain. Với nhu cầu \"mang ngữ cảnh tenant theo request\", dùng `AsyncLocal<T>` thay thế — đúng cơ chế mà bài logging của môn học dùng cho `ILogger.BeginScope`/`LogContext.PushProperty`: bọc nó trong một `TenantAccessor` static nhỏ quanh `AsyncLocal<string?>`, để `TenantMiddleware` gán `TenantAccessor.TenantId = tenantId` một lần trước khi gọi `await _next(httpContext)`, và để `LoggerService`/`OrderService` đọc từ accessor static đó thay vì nhận `TenantContext` qua constructor. Khi không còn service nào phụ thuộc vào một type Scoped, `TenantContext` có thể xoá hẳn, còn `LoggerService`/`OrderService` trở lại `AddSingleton` — container resolve chúng một lần, throughput hồi phục, mà ngữ cảnh tenant vẫn chảy đúng qua mọi `await` vì `AsyncLocal<T>` chụp `ExecutionContext` của đúng logical call, không phải thread vật lý. Đừng dừng ở \"code đọc thấy hợp lý\" — phải xác minh bằng load test trước/sau: chạy lại cùng load profile trên bản singleton + `AsyncLocal` và xác nhận p99 lẫn throughput thật sự quay về baseline.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given code runs correctly but is slow because of DI lifetime, not business logic. Reading it: `TenantContext` is registered with `builder.Services.AddScoped<TenantContext>()` to hold per-request `TenantId`; `LoggerService` takes a `TenantContext` through its constructor, so registering `LoggerService` as `AddSingleton` would throw at container-validation time: `InvalidOperationException: Cannot consume scoped service 'TenantContext' from singleton 'LoggerService'` — ASP.NET Core's built-in container validates scopes and refuses to let a longer-lived singleton hold a shorter-lived scoped dependency (exactly the \"captive dependency\" edge case from the DI lesson). To make the app even start, the team had no choice but to bump `LoggerService` to `AddScoped` too — and then `OrderService`, which depends on `LoggerService`, has to follow for the same reason. That's the C# shape of \"scope propagates up the whole chain\": ASP.NET Core doesn't silently auto-promote lifetimes the way NestJS does, but the validator forces the developer's hand one dependency at a time until the entire branch is Scoped. Where the real cost lives: ASP.NET Core creates a new DI scope for every incoming HTTP request, and under a Scoped registration the container builds a brand-new instance per scope — so every request now constructs `TenantContext`, `LoggerService`, AND `OrderService` from scratch instead of reusing the singletons that were resolved once at startup. That repeated construction across the whole branch, request after request, is exactly what doubles p99 and cuts throughput 40%, even though `CreateOrderAsync`'s business logic never changed. How to weigh it: Scoped should be the exception, reserved for state that is genuinely per-request and cannot be isolated any other way (like `DbContext` itself) — not reached for just to carry one string down a call chain. For \"carry tenant context per request\", use `AsyncLocal<T>` instead — the exact mechanism this course's logging lesson uses for `ILogger.BeginScope`/`LogContext.PushProperty`: wrap it in a small static `TenantAccessor` around `AsyncLocal<string?>`, have `TenantMiddleware` set `TenantAccessor.TenantId = tenantId` once before calling `await _next(httpContext)`, and let `LoggerService`/`OrderService` read from that static accessor instead of taking `TenantContext` through the constructor. With no service left depending on a Scoped type, `TenantContext` can be deleted entirely and `LoggerService`/`OrderService` go back to `AddSingleton` — the container resolves them once, throughput recovers, and tenant context still flows correctly through every `await` because `AsyncLocal<T>` captures the logical call's `ExecutionContext`, not the physical thread. Don't stop at \"the code reads right\" — validate with a before/after load test: run the same load profile against the singleton + `AsyncLocal` version and confirm p99 and throughput actually return to baseline."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCode cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng chậm không phải vì business logic đổi, mà vì cách mang tenant context. `LoggerService` và `OrderService` vốn được thiết kế là service dùng chung — tạo một lần ở composition root (`main`) và chia sẻ pointer, đúng tinh thần manual DI của Go — nhưng handler lại gọi `NewLoggerService(tenantCtx)` rồi `NewOrderService(loggerSvc)` bên trong mỗi request chỉ để nhét `TenantContext` vào field của `LoggerService`. Go không có IoC container để tự \"lan\" scope như NestJS/Spring, nhưng hệ quả tương đương vẫn xảy ra bằng tay: vì tenant context bị gắn làm state của struct, muốn có đúng tenant cho từng request thì buộc phải dựng lại cả nhánh phụ thuộc (`LoggerService` rồi `OrderService`) ngay trong handler, thay vì tái dùng đúng hai instance đã được wire một lần ở `main`. Chi phí thực nằm ở việc allocate và gọi lại hai constructor function trên mỗi request — bản chất giống hệt việc NestJS phải resolve lại cả nhánh request-scope, chỉ khác là ở Go bạn tự tay viết ra chỗ rebuild đó thay vì framework làm ngầm bên dưới. Cách sửa: đừng mang tenant theo struct field nữa — mang nó qua `context.Context`, đúng cơ chế Go dành riêng cho state gắn liền với một request: `ctx := context.WithValue(r.Context(), tenantKey, tenantID)`, rồi truyền `ctx` xuống làm tham số đầu của method (`Log(ctx context.Context, message string)`, `CreateOrder(ctx context.Context, dto CreateOrderDto)`). `LoggerService` và `OrderService` quay lại làm singleton được `main` tạo đúng một lần — pointer đó dùng chung cho mọi request, còn tenant context chỉ \"đi nhờ\" qua tham số `ctx` chứ không nằm trong struct nữa. Đừng dừng ở việc đọc code thấy sạch hơn — xác minh bằng load test trước/sau: chạy cùng load profile trên bản constructor-per-request và bản singleton + `context.Context`, xác nhận p99 lẫn throughput thật sự quay về baseline.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given code runs correctly but is slow, not because the business logic changed but because of how tenant context is carried. `LoggerService` and `OrderService` were meant to be shared services — created once at the composition root (`main`) and shared by pointer, in the spirit of Go's manual DI — but the handler calls `NewLoggerService(tenantCtx)` then `NewOrderService(loggerSvc)` inside every request just to stuff `TenantContext` into a `LoggerService` field. Go has no IoC container to automatically \"propagate\" scope the way NestJS/Spring does, but the same consequence happens by hand: because tenant context is baked in as struct state, getting the right tenant per request forces rebuilding the whole dependency branch (`LoggerService` then `OrderService`) right inside the handler, instead of reusing the two instances already wired once at `main`. The real cost is allocating and re-invoking both constructor functions on every request — the same underlying nature as NestJS having to re-resolve the whole request-scoped branch, except in Go you write out the rebuild by hand instead of the framework doing it invisibly. The fix: stop carrying tenant through a struct field — carry it through `context.Context`, Go's actual mechanism for state tied to a single request: `ctx := context.WithValue(r.Context(), tenantKey, tenantID)`, then pass `ctx` down as the first parameter of each method (`Log(ctx context.Context, message string)`, `CreateOrder(ctx context.Context, dto CreateOrderDto)`). `LoggerService` and `OrderService` go back to being singletons created exactly once by `main` — that same pointer is shared by every request, and tenant context just \"rides along\" through the `ctx` parameter instead of living in the struct. Don't stop at the code reading cleaner — validate with a before/after load test: run the same load profile against the constructor-per-request version and the singleton + `context.Context` version, and confirm p99 and throughput actually return to baseline."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation', '231d8cc57-stem1', '0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation/13', 13, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'design-lite', ARRAY['structured-logging', 'transport-fan-out', 'observability']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"2 giờ sáng production lỗi, bạn cần truy một request cụ thể giữa hàng triệu dòng log để lọc theo level và requestId. Hệ thống hiện tại chỉ rải các lệnh in log dạng chuỗi tự do khắp nơi (mỗi chỗ một kiểu message, bất kể stack — console.log, fmt.Println, System.out.println, Console.WriteLine...). Hãy trình bày thiết kế pipeline logging của bạn: vừa truy vấn được bằng máy vừa dễ đọc cho người trên terminal, và bảo vệ rõ vì sao MỘT sự kiện log lại nên fan-out ra nhiều đích với format khác nhau thay vì ép cả hệ thống dùng một format duy nhất.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"It's 2 a.m., production is failing, and you need to trace one specific request among millions of log lines by filtering on level and requestId. The current system just scatters free-form string log statements everywhere (a different message shape at every call site, whatever the stack — console.log, fmt.Println, System.out.println, Console.WriteLine...). Present your design of the logging pipeline: make it both machine-queryable and human-readable on the terminal, and defend clearly why ONE log event should fan out to multiple destinations with different formats instead of forcing the whole system onto a single format.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["`requestId` xuyên suốt một request đến từ đâu và được gắn ở tầng nào của vòng đời để mọi dòng log của cùng request đều mang nó?","Nếu file transport gặp filesystem read-only hoặc disk đầy, làm sao để logging không trở thành điểm chết của service?"],"hints":["Hỏi: máy *tìm kiếm* trên log kiểu gì? Nó cần field hay cần một câu chữ? Một dòng log chuỗi thuần \"user 5 failed\" cho máy lọc theo `level`/`requestId` được không?","Người cần màu-dễ-đọc, máy cần JSON-truy-vấn. Thay vì chọn một, hình dung một sự kiện được đẩy tới nhiều đích, mỗi đích tự serialize theo format của nó."]},"en":{"followUps":["Where does the request-wide `requestId` come from, and at which lifecycle layer is it attached so every log line of the same request carries it?","If the file transport hits a read-only filesystem or a full disk, how do you keep logging from becoming a single point of failure for the service?"],"hints":["Ask: how does a machine *search* logs? Does it want fields or a sentence? Can a bare string log \"user 5 failed\" be filtered by `level`/`requestId`?","Humans want color-readable, machines want JSON-queryable. Instead of choosing one, picture a single event pushed to multiple destinations, each serializing in its own format."]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Chỉ ra vấn đề của log chuỗi tự do (in thẳng một câu chữ, bất kể ngôn ngữ): không có field, aggregator phải regex để đoán field và vỡ khi message đổi chữ — không lọc được theo `level`/`requestId`, nên \"mò kim đáy bể\" lúc 2 giờ sáng.","Đề xuất structured logging: mỗi log entry là một bản ghi có field (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, và `requestId`/`userId`) serialize thành JSON một dòng để máy index/lọc/cảnh báo được.","Giải mâu thuẫn người-đọc vs máy-đọc bằng fan-out ra nhiều đích: logger giữ nhiều đích ra (tuỳ stack gọi là transport/sink/handler/appender), một sự kiện được đẩy tới *mọi* đích, mỗi đích có format riêng — một đích in ra terminal có màu dễ đọc cho người, một đích JSON cho aggregator/máy.","Nêu vì sao KHÔNG ép một format duy nhất: một format thì hoặc dev khổ (đọc JSON thô) hoặc máy khổ (parse chuỗi màu ANSI); fan-out cho cùng một dữ liệu hai cách trình bày mà không phải log hai lần.","(Nâng cao) Đề cập unified logger — gộp cả log nội bộ framework lẫn log app vào một pipeline để `requestId` xuyên suốt và trace không đứt đoạn ở ranh giới framework/app; và tránh pretty format ở file/production sink (tốn CPU, khó parse)."],"checklist":[{"text":"The root problem is free-form string logging: it has no fields, so an aggregator (Loki/ELK) must regex-guess each part and breaks the instant the message wording changes — it can't answer \"every log with `level=error` for `requestId=X` in the last 5 minutes\".","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Structured logging: each log entry is a record with fields (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, plus `requestId`/`userId`) serialized as one-line JSON, so machines can index and filter by field.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Transport fan-out: the logger holds several outputs and pushes one event to every output, each with its own format chain — one destination prints colored lines for devs to skim on the terminal, another emits plain JSON for the aggregator.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Why not force a single format: with only one format either devs suffer (reading raw JSON by eye) or machines suffer (parsing ANSI-colored strings); fan-out gives the same data two presentations without calling log twice.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A unified logger merges framework/runtime-internal logs (route mapping, lifecycle, errors) and application logs into one pipeline, so a single `requestId` runs end-to-end from route match to business event and the trace isn't broken at the framework/app boundary.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"Don't enable pretty format on the file/production sink, because the aggregator would swallow ANSI codes and fail to parse, and it wastes CPU — each transport gets the format right for its destination.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":13}],"keywords":["structured logging","transport fan-out","requestId","log aggregator","unified logger"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core problem is free-form string logging — every call site prints a different shaped sentence, so an aggregator like Loki has to regex-guess fields and breaks the moment wording changes, and you can't filter by level and requestId, which is exactly what you need at 2am. I'd redesign around structured logging first — each entry is a record with fields like level, message, context, timestamp, requestId, serialized as one-line JSON so it's queryable. Second, transport fan-out — the logger pushes one event to multiple destinations, each with its own format: colored readable lines on the terminal for devs, plain JSON to the file or aggregator for machines, so you're not forced to pick one format that makes either humans or machines suffer. Third, I'd unify framework-internal logs — routing, lifecycle — with application logs into the same pipeline so a single requestId traces the whole thing end to end. And I'd make sure pretty or colored format is only on the console sink, never on the file or production sink, because ANSI codes break parsing and waste CPU there.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The issue is that free-form logs don't have consistent fields, so machines can't filter on them reliably. I'd move to structured logging — JSON logs with fields like level, message, timestamp, and a requestId so you can trace one request. I'd also have multiple outputs — one for the console that's human-readable, and one for a file or log aggregator that's JSON — so both use cases are covered without picking just one format. I'd also try to keep the requestId consistent through the framework's own internal logs and the application logs so tracing isn't broken.","sonnet":67,"haiku":83},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the fix is just to always log in JSON instead of plain strings, so it's easier to search. Maybe have a requestId attached to logs so you can filter by that. For the terminal you'd probably still want it readable though, so maybe there's some way to format it differently there versus what gets saved to a file.","sonnet":0,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd probably just add more detail to the log messages, like including the request id in the string. And maybe use a logging library instead of console.log directly.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I think this is more of an infrastructure problem, you'd just buy a logging tool like Datadog and it handles the parsing for you regardless of the format, so the code doesn't really need to change.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Points out the problem with free-form string logging (printing a bare sentence, whatever the language): no fields, so an aggregator must regex-guess fields and breaks the moment the message wording changes — you can't filter by `level`/`requestId`, hence \"finding a needle in a haystack\" at 2 a.m.","Proposes structured logging: each log entry is a record with fields (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, plus `requestId`/`userId`) serialized as one-line JSON so machines can index/filter/alert on it.","Resolves the human-reads vs machine-reads tension with fan-out to multiple destinations: the logger holds several outputs (called transport/sink/handler/appender depending on the stack), one event is pushed to *every* output, each with its own format — one destination prints colored, human-readable lines to the terminal, another emits JSON for the aggregator/machine.","Explains why NOT a single format: one format means either devs suffer (reading raw JSON) or machines suffer (parsing ANSI-colored strings); fan-out gives the same data two presentations without logging twice.","(Advanced) Mentions a unified logger — merging framework-internal logs and app logs into one pipeline so a `requestId` runs end-to-end and the trace isn't broken at the framework/app boundary; and avoids pretty format on the file/production sink (CPU cost, hard to parse)."],"checklist":[{"text":"The root problem is free-form string logging: it has no fields, so an aggregator (Loki/ELK) must regex-guess each part and breaks the instant the message wording changes — it can't answer \"every log with `level=error` for `requestId=X` in the last 5 minutes\".","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Structured logging: each log entry is a record with fields (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, plus `requestId`/`userId`) serialized as one-line JSON, so machines can index and filter by field.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Transport fan-out: the logger holds several outputs and pushes one event to every output, each with its own format chain — one destination prints colored lines for devs to skim on the terminal, another emits plain JSON for the aggregator.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":20},{"text":"Why not force a single format: with only one format either devs suffer (reading raw JSON by eye) or machines suffer (parsing ANSI-colored strings); fan-out gives the same data two presentations without calling log twice.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A unified logger merges framework/runtime-internal logs (route mapping, lifecycle, errors) and application logs into one pipeline, so a single `requestId` runs end-to-end from route match to business event and the trace isn't broken at the framework/app boundary.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"Don't enable pretty format on the file/production sink, because the aggregator would swallow ANSI codes and fail to parse, and it wastes CPU — each transport gets the format right for its destination.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":13}],"keywords":["structured logging","transport fan-out","requestId","log aggregator","unified logger"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core problem is free-form string logging — every call site prints a different shaped sentence, so an aggregator like Loki has to regex-guess fields and breaks the moment wording changes, and you can't filter by level and requestId, which is exactly what you need at 2am. I'd redesign around structured logging first — each entry is a record with fields like level, message, context, timestamp, requestId, serialized as one-line JSON so it's queryable. Second, transport fan-out — the logger pushes one event to multiple destinations, each with its own format: colored readable lines on the terminal for devs, plain JSON to the file or aggregator for machines, so you're not forced to pick one format that makes either humans or machines suffer. Third, I'd unify framework-internal logs — routing, lifecycle — with application logs into the same pipeline so a single requestId traces the whole thing end to end. And I'd make sure pretty or colored format is only on the console sink, never on the file or production sink, because ANSI codes break parsing and waste CPU there.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The issue is that free-form logs don't have consistent fields, so machines can't filter on them reliably. I'd move to structured logging — JSON logs with fields like level, message, timestamp, and a requestId so you can trace one request. I'd also have multiple outputs — one for the console that's human-readable, and one for a file or log aggregator that's JSON — so both use cases are covered without picking just one format. I'd also try to keep the requestId consistent through the framework's own internal logs and the application logs so tracing isn't broken.","sonnet":67,"haiku":83},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the fix is just to always log in JSON instead of plain strings, so it's easier to search. Maybe have a requestId attached to logs so you can filter by that. For the terminal you'd probably still want it readable though, so maybe there's some way to format it differently there versus what gets saved to a file.","sonnet":0,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd probably just add more detail to the log messages, like including the request id in the string. And maybe use a logging library instead of console.log directly.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I think this is more of an infrastructure problem, you'd just buy a logging tool like Datadog and it handles the parsing for you regardless of the format, so the code doesn't really need to change.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nGốc vấn đề là log chuỗi tự do (in thẳng một câu chữ, bất kể ngôn ngữ): nó không có field, nên aggregator (Loki/ELK) phải regex đoán từng phần và vỡ ngay khi message đổi chữ — không thể lọc \"mọi log `level=error` của `requestId=X` trong 5 phút qua\", đúng thứ cần lúc 2 giờ sáng. Tôi thiết kế lại theo ba trụ. Thứ nhất, structured logging: mỗi log entry là một bản ghi có field (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, kèm `requestId`/`userId`) được serialize thành JSON một dòng, để máy index và lọc theo field. Thứ hai, transport fan-out để giải mâu thuẫn \"người cần dễ đọc / máy cần truy vấn\": logger giữ một mảng transport và đẩy *một* sự kiện tới *mọi* transport, mỗi transport có chuỗi format riêng — một đích in ra terminal có màu cho dev đọc nhanh, một đích JSON thuần cho aggregator. Đây là lý do KHÔNG ép một format duy nhất: nếu chỉ giữ một format thì hoặc dev khổ (đọc JSON thô bằng mắt), hoặc máy khổ (parse chuỗi có mã màu ANSI); fan-out cho cùng một dữ liệu hai cách trình bày mà không phải gọi log hai lần. Thứ ba, một unified logger: gộp cả log nội bộ framework (route mapping, lifecycle, exception) lẫn log application vào chung một pipeline, để một `requestId` xuyên suốt từ lúc route khớp tới business event và trace không đứt ở ranh giới framework/app. Lưu ý: không bật pretty format ở file/production sink vì aggregator sẽ nuốt cả ANSI và parse hỏng, lại tốn CPU — mỗi transport một format đúng đích của nó.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe root problem is free-form string logging (printing a bare sentence, whatever the language): it has no fields, so an aggregator (Loki/ELK) must regex-guess each part and breaks the instant the message wording changes — it can't answer \"every log with `level=error` for `requestId=X` in the last 5 minutes\", exactly what you need at 2 a.m. I'd redesign around three pillars. First, structured logging: each log entry is a record with fields (`level`, `message`, `context`, `timestamp`, plus `requestId`/`userId`) serialized as one-line JSON, so machines can index and filter by field. Second, transport fan-out to resolve the \"humans want readable / machines want queryable\" tension: the logger holds several outputs (transport/sink/handler depending on the stack) and pushes *one* event to *every* output, each with its own format chain — one destination prints colored lines for devs to skim on the terminal, another emits plain JSON for the aggregator. This is why you should NOT force a single format: with only one format either devs suffer (reading raw JSON by eye) or machines suffer (parsing ANSI-colored strings); fan-out gives the same data two presentations without calling log twice. Third, a unified logger: merge framework/runtime-internal logs (route mapping, lifecycle, errors) and application logs into one pipeline, so a single `requestId` runs end-to-end from route match to business event and the trace isn't broken at the framework/app boundary. Note: don't enable pretty format on the file/production sink, because the aggregator would swallow ANSI codes and fail to parse, and it wastes CPU — each transport gets the format right for its destination."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation', '231d8cc57-stem1', '0-fullstack-mastery/0-framework-foundation/14', 14, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'design-lite', ARRAY['error-handling', 'error-envelope', 'safe-by-default', 'centralized-filter']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Bạn được giao thiết kế lớp xử lý lỗi cho một backend production phục vụ nhiều loại client. Hãy trình bày thiết kế của bạn: vì sao mọi lỗi từ mọi tầng nên dồn về MỘT điểm tập trung duy nhất; bạn phân loại lỗi 'đoán-trước-được' (4xx) và lỗi 'bất ngờ' (5xx) ra sao; và vì sao nguyên tắc 'mặc định che. Error envelope bạn trả ra gồm những field nào, và vì sao path/timestamp không chỉ là trang trí?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"You are asked to design the error-handling layer for a production backend serving many kinds of clients. Present your design: why every error from every layer should converge on ONE central point; how you classify 'expected' errors (4xx) versus 'unexpected' ones (5xx); and why the principle 'hide by default. What fields does your error envelope return, and why are path/timestamp not just decoration?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Lỗi phát sinh từ background job hoặc code async không còn HTTP context sẽ không đi qua filter — bạn xử lý nhất quán envelope cho chúng thế nào?","Một `BadRequestException` (4xx) đặt email người dùng hoặc tên cột DB vào message cũng là lộ thông tin — bạn đặt luật gì cho message client-facing?"],"hints":["Hỏi: nếu mỗi controller tự bắt lỗi, ai đảm bảo mọi endpoint trả cùng một shape, và ai đảm bảo không endpoint nào quên che stack?","So hai chính sách: \"mặc định che, phải chủ đích lộ\" và \"mặc định lộ, phải nhớ che\". Trong chính sách nào thì một loại lỗi MỚI chưa lường trước vẫn tự động an toàn?"]},"en":{"followUps":["Errors from a background job or async code with no HTTP context won't pass through the filter — how do you keep a consistent envelope for them?","A `BadRequestException` (4xx) that puts a user's email or a DB column name into the message is also a leak — what rule do you set for client-facing messages?"],"hints":["Ask: if each controller catches its own errors, who guarantees every endpoint returns the same shape, and who guarantees no endpoint forgets to hide the stack?","Compare two policies: \"hide by default, expose deliberately\" and \"expose by default, remember to hide\". In which policy is a NEW, unanticipated error type still automatically safe?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Nêu được ý tưởng gom lỗi về MỘT điểm xử lý lỗi tập trung mà mọi request đi qua — exception filter/handler toàn cục ở stack có exception (Nest/Spring/ASP.NET), hoặc error-handling middleware + `recover()` ở Go — thay vì mỗi handler tự bắt lỗi tại chỗ, và biết một error envelope là một shape nhất quán trả cho client.","Giải thích được cơ chế khiến điểm tập trung khả thi: nó nằm \"ngoài đường thẳng\" của vòng đời, nên bất kỳ tầng nào phát sinh lỗi (ném exception, hoặc trả error được middleware bắt lại) đều được định tuyến thẳng tới nó — nghĩa là envelope chỉ định nghĩa một lần vẫn nhất quán trên toàn hệ thống.","Phân loại đúng: lỗi \"đoán-trước-được\" được báo hiệu chủ đích (ném một exception có kiểu, hoặc trả một domain error đã phân loại) và map sang 4xx, còn lỗi \"bất ngờ\" là mọi thứ còn lại và rơi xuống 5xx.","Lập luận được safe-by-default: nhánh catch-all / panic-recovery là lưới hứng cuối — bất cứ lỗi nào KHÔNG được phân loại là expected tự trở thành 500-generic và che nội bộ, nên việc lộ một message là ngoại lệ phải khai báo có chủ đích, không bao giờ là hành vi mặc định.","Nêu được tên các field của envelope — `statusCode`/`message`/`error` cùng `path` và `timestamp` — và giải thích `path`/`timestamp` là mảnh ghép để đối chiếu báo lỗi client với đúng dòng log server, không phải trang trí."],"checklist":[{"text":"Every error from every layer should converge on ONE central error-handling point — in the request lifecycle this point sits \"off the straight line\", so any layer that produces an error makes the flow jump straight there, skipping the rest; the exact mechanism differs by stack (a global exception filter/handler for exception-based frameworks like Nest/Spring/ASP.NET vs. an error-handling middleware plus `recover()` and a shared response writer for Go) but the idea is identical: exactly one exit for errors.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Because every error is guaranteed to pass through this one point, shaping the response there gives immediate system-wide consistency; if each controller catches and formats on its own, clients get a different shape per endpoint and someone eventually leaks a stack.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Expected errors (usually 4xx — not found, bad input, conflict, unauthenticated) belong to the client: their message is written deliberately by the domain, so it's safe for the client to read and the central point keeps it as-is.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Unexpected errors (5xx — a programming error, DB down, broken invariant) belong to the server: their real message carries a stack/SQL string that's unsafe to expose, so the central point replaces it with a generic message and pushes the real stack only into internal logs for on-call.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Safe-by-default: the final catch-all/panic-recovery branch is the last net, so even a never-anticipated new error type is automatically hidden — hiding is the default behavior, exposing is the exception you must declare deliberately.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Doing the reverse — exposing by default and remembering to hide each error type — means forgetting one branch leaks internals, and you can never be sure you've hidden them all.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The envelope is a contract — `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` (ideally plus `requestId`) — that lets the caller write one error-handling code path instead of guessing the shape per endpoint.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"`path` and `timestamp` are not decoration: they map a client's error report to the exact server log line, turning a vague \"the API returned 500\" into a traceable incident.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["centralized exception filter","error envelope","expected vs unexpected","safe-by-default","error contract"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The whole design comes down to one point, one envelope. Every error should converge on a single central point — in most exception-based frameworks that's a global exception filter that every throw eventually lands in, since the error path actually sits off the normal sequential line and jumps there from any layer. In Go without exceptions you'd get the same effect with error-handling middleware plus a recover for panics. Because everything is guaranteed to pass through that one point, you can shape the response once and get consistency, instead of every controller formatting its own thing and eventually leaking a stack somewhere. For classification, expected errors are usually 4xx — not found, bad input, conflict — they belong to the client, the message was written deliberately by the domain so it's safe to show. Unexpected ones are 5xx — a null pointer, DB down, broken invariant — their real message might have a stack trace or SQL in it, so the central point swaps it for a generic message and only logs the real detail internally. Hiding by default is safer because the catch-all branch is the last net — anything not explicitly classified as expected just falls into a generic 500 and gets hidden automatically; if you did it the other way, expose by default, you'd have to remember to hide every single type and one missed branch leaks internals. The envelope itself is something like statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path, maybe requestId — and path and timestamp aren't just decoration, they're literally what lets you map a client's bug report back to the exact log line on the server.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd want all errors to go through one central exception handler rather than each controller catching its own errors, so the response shape stays consistent everywhere. I'd split errors into expected ones, like 404 or 400, which are safe to show the real message for, and unexpected ones, like a crash or DB failure, where I'd just return a generic message and log the actual error internally. Defaulting to hiding the message is safer because if you forget to classify a new error type, it just falls back to hidden instead of leaking something sensitive. The response body would have something like statusCode, message, timestamp, and the path, so you can trace which request produced which error in the logs.","sonnet":75,"haiku":100},{"level":3,"answer":"I'd have some kind of centralized error handler so errors don't all get handled differently in each route. I think you'd want to hide internal error details from the client, like database errors, and just show something generic. Client errors like 400 or 404 are probably okay to show as-is since the user caused them. The response probably needs a message and maybe a status code.","sonnet":25,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd just wrap the routes in try-catch and return an error message if something fails. For serious errors I guess you'd just say something went wrong instead of the real error.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I think each endpoint should just handle its own errors since they know their own context best, a global handler might not know what message makes sense for a specific route.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["States the idea of funneling errors to ONE central error-handling point every request passes through — a globally-registered exception filter/handler on exception-based stacks (Nest/Spring/ASP.NET), or error-handling middleware + `recover()` on Go — instead of each handler catching on its own, and knows an error envelope is one consistent shape returned to the client.","Explains the mechanism that makes one central point feasible: it sits \"off the straight line\" of the lifecycle, so any layer that produces an error (throws an exception, or returns an error the middleware catches) is routed straight to it — meaning the envelope, defined once, stays consistent system-wide.","Classifies errors correctly: \"expected\" ones are signalled deliberately (a typed exception thrown, or a classified domain error returned) and map to 4xx, while \"unexpected\" ones are anything else and fall through to 5xx.","Argues safe-by-default: the catch-all / panic-recovery branch is the final net — any error NOT classified as expected becomes a generic 500 with internals hidden, so exposing a message is a deliberate, declared exception, never the default behavior.","Names the envelope fields — `statusCode`/`message`/`error` plus `path` and `timestamp` — and explains `path`/`timestamp` are debugging anchors that let you correlate the client's error report with the exact server log line, not decoration."],"checklist":[{"text":"Every error from every layer should converge on ONE central error-handling point — in the request lifecycle this point sits \"off the straight line\", so any layer that produces an error makes the flow jump straight there, skipping the rest; the exact mechanism differs by stack (a global exception filter/handler for exception-based frameworks like Nest/Spring/ASP.NET vs. an error-handling middleware plus `recover()` and a shared response writer for Go) but the idea is identical: exactly one exit for errors.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Because every error is guaranteed to pass through this one point, shaping the response there gives immediate system-wide consistency; if each controller catches and formats on its own, clients get a different shape per endpoint and someone eventually leaks a stack.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Expected errors (usually 4xx — not found, bad input, conflict, unauthenticated) belong to the client: their message is written deliberately by the domain, so it's safe for the client to read and the central point keeps it as-is.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Unexpected errors (5xx — a programming error, DB down, broken invariant) belong to the server: their real message carries a stack/SQL string that's unsafe to expose, so the central point replaces it with a generic message and pushes the real stack only into internal logs for on-call.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Safe-by-default: the final catch-all/panic-recovery branch is the last net, so even a never-anticipated new error type is automatically hidden — hiding is the default behavior, exposing is the exception you must declare deliberately.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Doing the reverse — exposing by default and remembering to hide each error type — means forgetting one branch leaks internals, and you can never be sure you've hidden them all.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The envelope is a contract — `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` (ideally plus `requestId`) — that lets the caller write one error-handling code path instead of guessing the shape per endpoint.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"`path` and `timestamp` are not decoration: they map a client's error report to the exact server log line, turning a vague \"the API returned 500\" into a traceable incident.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["centralized exception filter","error envelope","expected vs unexpected","safe-by-default","error contract"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The whole design comes down to one point, one envelope. Every error should converge on a single central point — in most exception-based frameworks that's a global exception filter that every throw eventually lands in, since the error path actually sits off the normal sequential line and jumps there from any layer. In Go without exceptions you'd get the same effect with error-handling middleware plus a recover for panics. Because everything is guaranteed to pass through that one point, you can shape the response once and get consistency, instead of every controller formatting its own thing and eventually leaking a stack somewhere. For classification, expected errors are usually 4xx — not found, bad input, conflict — they belong to the client, the message was written deliberately by the domain so it's safe to show. Unexpected ones are 5xx — a null pointer, DB down, broken invariant — their real message might have a stack trace or SQL in it, so the central point swaps it for a generic message and only logs the real detail internally. Hiding by default is safer because the catch-all branch is the last net — anything not explicitly classified as expected just falls into a generic 500 and gets hidden automatically; if you did it the other way, expose by default, you'd have to remember to hide every single type and one missed branch leaks internals. The envelope itself is something like statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path, maybe requestId — and path and timestamp aren't just decoration, they're literally what lets you map a client's bug report back to the exact log line on the server.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd want all errors to go through one central exception handler rather than each controller catching its own errors, so the response shape stays consistent everywhere. I'd split errors into expected ones, like 404 or 400, which are safe to show the real message for, and unexpected ones, like a crash or DB failure, where I'd just return a generic message and log the actual error internally. Defaulting to hiding the message is safer because if you forget to classify a new error type, it just falls back to hidden instead of leaking something sensitive. The response body would have something like statusCode, message, timestamp, and the path, so you can trace which request produced which error in the logs.","sonnet":75,"haiku":100},{"level":3,"answer":"I'd have some kind of centralized error handler so errors don't all get handled differently in each route. I think you'd want to hide internal error details from the client, like database errors, and just show something generic. Client errors like 400 or 404 are probably okay to show as-is since the user caused them. The response probably needs a message and maybe a status code.","sonnet":25,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd just wrap the routes in try-catch and return an error message if something fails. For serious errors I guess you'd just say something went wrong instead of the real error.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I think each endpoint should just handle its own errors since they know their own context best, a global handler might not know what message makes sense for a specific route.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nThiết kế xoay quanh một câu: một điểm, một envelope. Mọi lỗi từ mọi tầng nên dồn về MỘT điểm xử lý lỗi tập trung, vì trong vòng đời request tầng xử lý lỗi nằm \"ngoài đường thẳng\" — bất kỳ tầng nào phát sinh lỗi đều khiến luồng nhảy thẳng tới điểm đó, bỏ qua phần còn lại. Về cơ chế thì tuỳ stack: nơi có exception (Nest/Spring/ASP.NET) dùng một exception filter/handler toàn cục mà mọi `throw` rơi vào; Go không có exception nên dùng một error-handling middleware bọc mọi handler cộng `recover()` cho panic, rồi một hàm ghi response lỗi dùng chung — khác cú pháp nhưng cùng một ý: đúng một cửa ra cho lỗi. Đã chắc *mọi* lỗi đi qua đây thì chỉ cần định hình response một lần là có ngay tính nhất quán cho toàn hệ thống; nếu để mỗi controller tự bắt và tự format, client sẽ nhận mỗi endpoint một shape, và dễ vô tình lộ stack. Phân loại: lỗi *đoán-trước-được* (expected, thường 4xx) thuộc về client — không tìm thấy (404), input sai (400), xung đột (409), chưa xác thực (401); message do domain chủ động viết nên an toàn để client đọc, filter giữ nguyên. Lỗi *bất ngờ* (unexpected, 5xx) thuộc về server — lỗi lập trình (vd `TypeError`/nil-panic), DB rớt, invariant vỡ; message thật của chúng chứa stack/chuỗi SQL, không an toàn để lộ, nên điểm tập trung thay bằng một message generic (\"Internal server error\") và chỉ đẩy stack vào log nội bộ cho on-call. Vì sao safe-by-default an toàn hơn: nhánh \"còn lại\" (catch-all / panic-recovery) là lưới hứng cuối — bất cứ lỗi nào KHÔNG được phân loại là expected tự rơi vào 500-generic, nên một loại lỗi mới chưa từng lường trước cũng *tự động* bị che. Che là hành vi mặc định; *lộ* mới là ngoại lệ phải khai báo có chủ đích, bằng cách cố ý báo hiệu một lỗi expected có kiểu cho lỗi đoán-trước. Làm ngược lại — mặc định lộ, phải nhớ che từng loại — thì chỉ cần quên một nhánh là rò nội bộ, và bạn không bao giờ chắc đã che hết. Envelope là một contract: `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` (lý tưởng thêm `requestId`) cho phép phía gọi viết *một* code-path xử lý lỗi thay vì đoán shape theo từng endpoint. `path` và `timestamp` không phải trang trí — chúng là mảnh ghép để map một báo lỗi của client về đúng dòng log server, biến một \"API trả 500\" mơ hồ thành một sự cố truy được. Bản chất: error handling tốt là để lại đủ dấu vết bên trong và vừa đủ thông tin bên ngoài.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe design turns on one phrase: one point, one envelope. Every error from every layer should converge on ONE central error-handling point, because in the request lifecycle the error-handling layer sits \"off the straight line\" — any layer that produces an error makes the flow jump straight to that point, skipping the rest. The mechanism varies by stack: exception-based ones (Nest/Spring/ASP.NET) use a global exception filter/handler that every `throw` lands in; Go has no exceptions, so it uses an error-handling middleware wrapping every handler plus `recover()` for panics and one shared error-response writer — different syntax, same idea: exactly one exit for errors. Since *every* error is guaranteed to pass through here, shaping the response once gives immediate system-wide consistency; if each controller catches and formats on its own, clients get a different shape per endpoint and someone eventually leaks a stack. Classification: *expected* errors (usually 4xx) belong to the client — not found (404), bad input (400), conflict (409), unauthenticated (401); their message is written deliberately by the domain, so it's safe for the client to read and the filter keeps it. *Unexpected* errors (5xx) belong to the server — a programming error (e.g. `TypeError`/nil-panic), DB down, broken invariant; their real message carries a stack/SQL string, unsafe to expose, so the central point replaces it with a generic message (\"Internal server error\") and pushes only the stack into internal logs for on-call. Why safe-by-default is safer: the final catch-all / panic-recovery branch is the last net — any error NOT classified as expected falls into 500-generic, so even a never-anticipated new error type is *automatically* hidden. Hiding is the default behavior; *exposing* is the exception you must declare deliberately by intentionally signalling a typed expected error. Do the reverse — expose by default, remember to hide each type — and forgetting one branch leaks internals, and you're never sure you've hidden them all. The envelope is a contract: `{statusCode, error, message, timestamp, path}` (ideally plus `requestId`) lets the caller write *one* error-handling code path instead of guessing the shape per endpoint. `path` and `timestamp` are not decoration — they are the pieces that map a client's error report to the exact server log line, turning a vague \"the API returned 500\" into a traceable incident. In essence: good error handling leaves enough trace inside and just enough information outside."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
